window.BOOK_CYBERSECURITY_CHAPTERS = {
  "README.md": "# Cybersecurity & Digital Hygiene for Junior Coders 🛡️🔐\n\nWelcome to the official **Cybersecurity & Digital Hygiene Textbook** of the Junior Coders curriculum!\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Course Overview\n\nSecurity is not an afterthought added at the end of development; it is the foundation of digital trust. This textbook equips students to think like ethical defenders—understanding how attackers exploit human psychology, web code, and network packets, and building impenetrable defenses.\n\nIn this textbook, students will:\n\n1. **Adopt the Defender's Mindset**: Understand the CIA Triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability), Defense in Depth, and Islamic ethics of digital stewardship (*Amānah*).\n2. **Master Authentication & Passwords**: Password entropy calculations, GPU cracking mathematics, salted hashing with Argon2id/bcrypt, and Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).\n3. **Deconstruct Social Engineering**: Spot phishing lures, typosquatting domains, pretexting calls, spoofed email headers (SPF/DKIM), and psychological manipulation.\n4. **Defeat the OWASP Top 10**: Prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), SQL Injection (SQLi) via Prepared Statements, CSRF tokens, and Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR).\n5. **Differentiate Cryptographic Primitives**: Encoding vs Encryption vs Hashing, the Avalanche Effect, and public Wi-Fi protection via TLS 1.3 and VPN tunnels.\n6. **Practice Daily Digital Hygiene**: Device malware defenses, ransomware recovery, 3-2-1 backups, automatic patching, and EXIF metadata stripping.\n7. **Perform Incident Response**: The 6 phases of containment and recovery, digital forensics, and ethical vulnerability disclosure (*Ṣidq*).\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Curriculum Roadmap\n\n| Part | Focus | Chapters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Part 1** | Foundations of Security & Authentication | Ch 1 - Ch 2 |\n| **Part 2** | Social Engineering & Phishing Defense | Ch 3 - Ch 4 |\n| **Part 3** | Web Application Vulnerabilities (OWASP) & Security Headers | Ch 5 - Ch 9 |\n| **Part 4** | Cryptography & Secure Channels | Ch 10 - Ch 11 |\n| **Part 5** | Digital Hygiene & Privacy Protection | Ch 12 - Ch 13 |\n| **Part 6** | Incident Response & Capstone Hardening | Ch 14 - Ch 15 |\n\n---\n\n## 🌟 Islamic Ethical Framework (*Amānah & Ṣidq*)\n\nAll lessons in this curriculum adhere to our core ethical standards:\n\n- **Data Privacy as a Sacred Trust (*Amānah*)**: User data and sensitive communications must be guarded. Breaching or exposing private data without authorization violates this sacred trust.\n- **Truthfulness (*Ṣidq*)**: Absolute prohibition of deceptive social engineering, phishing, fraud, or fraudulent impersonation.\n- **Strictly Halal Contexts**: No malware creation, no unauthorized intrusion tutorials, no extortion simulators, and no surveillance tools targeting innocent individuals.\n- **Craftsmanship & Diligence (*Iḥsān*)**: Build secure architectures with thorough validation, defensive parameterization, and robust error handling.\n",
  "part-01-foundations-of-security/ch01-the-mindset-of-digital-defense.md": "# Chapter 1: The Mindset of Digital Defense 🛡️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nEvery evening, the caretaker of the neighbourhood library walks through the building before locking up. She checks that the rare-book room is locked. She checks that nobody has torn pages out of the reference books. She also makes sure the front door will open on time tomorrow morning. She is not chasing burglars; she is quietly protecting something many people depend on. That is exactly what a digital defender does.\n\nToday, medical records, school grades, charity funds and family messages are stored on computers. Welcome to **Cybersecurity & Digital Hygiene**! In this book you move from being a builder of code to being a guardian of digital trust (**Amānah**).\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain the three goals of security: **Confidentiality, Integrity and Availability** (the CIA Triad).\n- Tell the difference between an ethical defender and someone who breaks in without permission.\n- Describe **Defense in Depth**: many layers of protection instead of one.\n- Spot which security goal is at risk in an everyday situation.\n\n## 💡 The CIA Triad: Three Goals of Security\n\nImagine protecting a library of rare science manuscripts. You would care about three things.\n\n1. **Confidentiality** (keeping secrets): only allowed people can read private things. Strangers cannot peek at the research notes.\n2. **Integrity** (keeping things correct): nobody can secretly change a medicine dose or a date in a history book. If one letter changes, we must be able to notice.\n3. **Availability** (keeping things open): the library is open when people need to study. Someone blocking the doors must be stopped.\n\nIn computer security these three goals are called **the CIA Triad**. \"Triad\" simply means a group of three.\n\n```\n            ▲\n           / \\\n          / C \\    Confidentiality\n         /─────\\   (only allowed eyes)\n        /   I   \\  Integrity\n       /─────────\\ (no secret changes)\n      /     A     \\ Availability\n     ─────────────── (online when needed)\n```\n\n### Example: a school report-card portal\n\n| Goal | What could go wrong | A defence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Confidentiality | A classmate reads your marks | Each student has their own login |\n| Integrity | Someone changes a grade | Only teachers can edit marks; every change is logged |\n| Availability | The site crashes on results day | Backups and a server that can handle the crowd |\n\nNotice that each defence protects one goal. A good system protects all three.\n\n## ⚖️ Ethical Defenders and Attackers\n\nKnowing how systems break is powerful knowledge. What matters is how you use it.\n\n- **Ethical defender** (often called a \"white hat\"): uses security knowledge to protect people. They fix bugs and report problems to the owner.\n- **Criminal attacker** (often called a \"black hat\"): breaks into systems to steal, spy, or lock files and demand payment.\n- **\"Gray hat\"**: tests systems *without permission*, even when they mean no harm. This still crosses a line.\n\n> 🌟 **The Principle of Trust (*Amānah*)**: Private data is a trust placed in someone's care. Testing a website without written permission is like trying the locks on a neighbour's house \"just to check\". Even if you take nothing, you have broken their trust. Breaking into accounts or systems without permission is **illegal** and against school rules.\n\nSo where can you practise? Only on things you own, or on labs built for practice. This book's **Cybersecurity Lab Studio** is one of those safe places. If you ever find a weakness in a real site, tell the owner or a teacher. Chapter 14 shows you how.\n\n## 🧅 Defense in Depth (The Onion Model)\n\nNever trust a single barrier. A lock on the front door is good. A lock, a guard, a locked cupboard and a visitors' book is much better. If one fails, the others still protect you.\n\nComputer systems work the same way. Security people call this **Defense in Depth**: many layers, like the layers of an onion.\n\n```\n[ Network: firewall ]\n   │  (a firewall blocks unwanted\n   ▼   network traffic)\n[ Web: HTTPS encryption ]\n   │\n   ▼\n[ App: check every form input ]\n   │\n   ▼\n[ Login: password + second step ]\n   │\n   ▼\n[ Data: hashed passwords, backups ]\n```\n\nYou will meet each of these layers later in the book. For now, remember the idea: **if one layer fails, the next layer still stands.**\n\n### Example: one layer versus many\n\n- ❌ **Risky:** a charity website relies only on a strong admin password. One leaked password and everything is open.\n- ✅ **Better:** the same site also asks for a code from the admin's phone, lets only coordinators edit donor records, and keeps daily backups. A leaked password alone is no longer a disaster.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Use a different password for every important account (Chapter 2 shows how).\n- Turn on two-step login wherever it is offered.\n- Keep your devices updated.\n- Back up your schoolwork somewhere other than your laptop.\n- If something online feels wrong, stop and ask a trusted adult.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"My little school club site has nothing worth stealing, so security does not matter.\"\n\n**What happens:** attackers often use automatic tools that try thousands of sites. They do not pick you; they simply find you. A weak site can be filled with spam, used to trick visitors, or have its member list leaked.\n\n**Fixed thinking:** every site that holds names, emails or passwords holds someone's trust. Protect it with the basics: strong passwords, updates and backups.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nPick an app or website you use every day, such as a school learning portal. Write one sentence for each goal:\n\n- **C:** How does it keep your marks private?\n- **I:** How does it make sure only teachers can change a grade?\n- **A:** How does it stay online during exam week?\n\n*Hint:* think about logins, who is allowed to edit, and what happens when thousands of students visit at once.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Security protects three things: **Confidentiality, Integrity and Availability** (the CIA Triad).\n- Ethical defenders work only with permission and report problems to the owner.\n- Testing systems without permission is wrong and illegal, even if nothing is taken.\n- **Defense in Depth** means many layers, so one failure does not open everything.\n- Data that people give you is an *Amānah*: a trust you must protect.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat are the three goals of the CIA Triad? Give a short meaning for each.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**Confidentiality** (only allowed people can see data), **Integrity** (data is not changed secretly) and **Availability** (the system works when people need it).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nFor each event, name the CIA goal that was harmed:\n\n1. A hospital website is so overloaded that patients cannot book appointments.\n2. A student's attendance is changed from \"absent\" to \"present\" by someone who is not a teacher.\n3. A list of donors' home addresses is left on a public web page.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. **Availability**: the service could not be used when it was needed.\n2. **Integrity**: the data was changed without permission.\n3. **Confidentiality**: private data was seen by people who should not see it.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA community food bank keeps its volunteer schedule and family list on a small website. Design its defence in depth.\n\n1. List at least four layers of protection, from the outside in.\n2. For each layer, say which CIA goal it mainly protects.\n3. Explain what still protects the family list if the admin password is stolen.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **HTTPS** on the whole site: protects the confidentiality of data travelling over the network.\n2. **Password plus a phone code** for admins: protects confidentiality and integrity.\n3. **Roles**: volunteers see the schedule, but only coordinators see the family list: confidentiality.\n4. **A change log** recording who edited what: integrity.\n5. **Daily backups** kept somewhere else: availability and integrity.\n\nIf the admin password is stolen, the **phone code** still blocks the login. Even if that failed, the **change log** shows what was touched and the **backups** let the team restore correct data.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-foundations-of-security/ch02-authentication-passwords-and-mfa.md": "# Chapter 2: Authentication, Password Entropy & MFA 🔑\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBilal's school locker has a three-digit combination lock. That gives only 1,000 possible codes, from 000 to 999. A patient person could try them all in an afternoon. His bicycle lock has six digits: one million codes, which is much harder. And the school office does not trust a code alone. Visitors must also show a signed card at the desk. Longer secrets, plus a second check: that is this whole chapter in one story.\n\nMost break-ins online do not start with clever code. They start with a password that was guessed, stolen or reused.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Calculate password **entropy** and explain why length beats complexity.\n- Build a strong **passphrase** and explain why a password manager helps.\n- Explain why websites must store **hashes**, not passwords, and what a **salt** does.\n- Name the three kinds of login factors and explain **MFA**.\n\n## 🎲 Password Entropy: Measuring Unpredictability\n\n**Authentication** means proving who you are, for example by typing a password.\n\n**Entropy** measures how unpredictable a password is. It is counted in **bits**. Each extra bit doubles the number of guesses an attacker might need.\n\n> **Entropy = L × log₂(R)**\n> L = length (how many characters or words)\n> R = pool size (how many choices for each one)\n\nWhat is log₂(R)? It is \"how many times you double 1 to reach R\". For example, log₂(8) = 3, because 1 → 2 → 4 → 8. Here are the pools you will use:\n\n| Pool | R | log₂(R) (bits each) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Digits 0–9 | 10 | about 3.32 |\n| Lowercase a–z | 26 | about 4.70 |\n| All keyboard characters | 95 | about 6.57 |\n| A list of 7,776 words | 7,776 | about 12.9 |\n\n### Worked example\nA 6-digit PIN: 6 × 3.32 ≈ **19.9 bits**. Check: 2²⁰ = 1,048,576, close to the 1,000,000 possible PINs.\n\n> ⚠️ **Big rule:** the formula only works if the password is chosen **at random**. A human-chosen word like `sunshine` has 8 × 4.70 ≈ 37.6 bits on paper. But it sits on every list of common passwords, so it falls almost at once.\n\n### How long would guessing take?\nSuppose a stolen password list was stored with a fast hash. A strong computer might try about **10 billion guesses per second**. On average it finds the answer after searching half of all choices.\n\n| Password | How it was made | Entropy | Average time at 10 billion/s |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `123456` | Common password | (on every list) | Instant |\n| `sunshine` | Dictionary word | (on every list) | Instant |\n| `Tr$8!q2Z` | 8 random keyboard characters | 52.6 bits | About 4 days |\n| `lamp orbit cedar mild frost` | 5 random words from 7,776 | 64.6 bits | About 44 years |\n| 6 random words from 7,776 | 6 random words | 77.5 bits | About 340,000 years |\n\nSo **length beats complexity**. Five or six random words are easier to remember than `Tr$8!q2Z`, and far harder to guess. (Never use the example passphrase above: it is now printed in a book!)\n\n### A password manager\nYou cannot remember 40 different passphrases. A **password manager** is an app that stores them all in an encrypted vault. You remember one strong passphrase; it remembers the rest and creates new random passwords for you. This also stops **reuse**, where one leaked password unlocks many accounts.\n\n## 🧂 Storing Passwords: Hashes, Not Plain Text\n\nWhat if a website's database is stolen? It depends on how passwords were stored.\n\n- ❌ **Plain text** (`secret123`): the thief reads every password directly.\n- ❌ **A fast hash** like MD5 or SHA-256: a **hash** turns a password into a fixed \"fingerprint\" that cannot be turned back (Chapter 10 explains more). But fast hashes can be checked billions of times per second, so guessing is quick.\n- ✅ **A slow password hash** like **bcrypt** or **Argon2id**: each guess is made deliberately slow. With bcrypt at normal settings, a strong graphics card manages only thousands of guesses per second, instead of billions.\n\nPHP has this built in. `password_hash()` makes the slow hash and adds a salt for you. `password_verify()` checks a login attempt.\n\n```php\n<?php\n$hash = password_hash('olive trees bloom', PASSWORD_DEFAULT);\necho substr($hash, 0, 7), \" length \", strlen($hash), \"\\n\";\n\n$again = password_hash('olive trees bloom', PASSWORD_DEFAULT);\nvar_dump($hash === $again);\n\nvar_dump(password_verify('olive trees bloom', $hash));\nvar_dump(password_verify('olive trees bloom!', $hash));\n```\n\nOutput (PHP 8.3):\n\n```\n$2y$10$ length 60\nbool(false)\nbool(true)\nbool(false)\n```\n\n- `$2y$10$` means bcrypt with a work setting (\"cost\") of 10.\n- The same password hashed twice gives **different** hashes (`false`). That is the salt at work.\n- Only the exact password passes `password_verify()`.\n\n### What is a salt?\nA **salt** is a random value made for each user and mixed in before hashing. Two people with the same password get completely different hashes. It also defeats **rainbow tables**, which are huge ready-made lists of passwords and their hashes.\n\n## 📲 Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)\n\nThere are three kinds of login **factors**:\n\n1. **Something you know:** a password, PIN or passphrase.\n2. **Something you have:** your phone with an authenticator app, or a small hardware security key.\n3. **Something you are:** a fingerprint or face scan.\n\n**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)** asks for factors from **two or more different kinds**. **2FA** (two-factor) is the most common type.\n\n```\n[ password ] + [ 6-digit app code ]\n                (changes every 30 s)\n        │\n        ▼\n [ ACCESS GRANTED ]\n```\n\nIf someone steals your password, they still need your phone. A password plus a second password is **not** MFA, because both are \"something you know\".\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Make your main passwords passphrases of 5–6 random words.\n- Never reuse a password on two sites.\n- Use a password manager, with a passphrase you have never used anywhere else.\n- Turn on 2FA for email first. Your email can reset every other account.\n- Never tell anyone a login code, even someone who says they are from \"support\".\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"I made my password strong: `Ahmed2012!`\"\n\n**What happens:** it follows a pattern people use all the time: a name, a year and a symbol at the end. Guessing tools try exactly these patterns first, so the real strength is far below what the formula suggests.\n\n**Fixed version:** five random words, such as `kettle meadow orbit linen spark` (do not use this one). Random choice is what gives entropy, not clever-looking symbols.\n\n**Developer mistake:** storing `hash('sha256', $password)` in a database. Fixed version: `password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT)` and check with `password_verify()`.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen the **Password** tab in the Cybersecurity Lab Studio.\n\n1. Type a 6-digit number and note the estimated time.\n2. Type four or five random, unrelated words with spaces.\n3. Compare the entropy and the time.\n\n*Hint:* add one more random word and watch the bits grow by about 13.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Entropy = L × log₂(R), but only for randomly chosen passwords.\n- Length beats complexity: 5–6 random words are strong and memorable.\n- A password manager stops reuse, which is one of the biggest risks.\n- Websites must store slow, salted hashes (bcrypt or Argon2id), never plain text or plain SHA-256.\n- MFA needs two different kinds of factor; a phone code plus a password is the common form.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nName the three kinds of authentication factors, with one example of each.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- Something you **know**: a password or PIN.\n- Something you **have**: a phone with an authenticator app, or a security key.\n- Something you **are**: a fingerprint or face scan.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nUse Entropy = L × log₂(R) to compare two randomly chosen passwords:\n\n1. 10 random lowercase letters.\n2. 4 random words from a 7,776-word list.\n\nWhich is stronger, and by how many bits?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. 10 × 4.70 = **47.0 bits**.\n2. 4 × 12.9 = **51.7 bits**.\n\nThe four words are stronger by about **4.7 bits**, which means about 26 times as many possible choices (2 multiplied by itself 4.7 times is about 26). They are also much easier to remember.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA student club stores member passwords like this:\n\n```php\n$stored = hash('sha256', $_POST['password']);\n```\n\n1. Explain two problems with this line.\n2. Rewrite the line for signing up.\n3. Write the line that checks a password at login.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. SHA-256 is **fast**, so a stolen list can be guessed at billions of tries per second. There is **no salt**, so two members with the same password get the same hash, and ready-made tables work.\n2. Sign up:\n\n```php\n$stored = password_hash($_POST['password'], PASSWORD_DEFAULT);\n```\n\n3. Log in:\n\n```php\nif (password_verify($_POST['password'], $stored)) {\n    echo \"Welcome back!\";\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-social-engineering-and-phishing/ch03-social-engineering-the-human-hack.md": "# Chapter 3: Social Engineering — The Human Hack 🎭\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA school spends a lot of money on a strong gate, card readers and cameras. One morning a smiling man arrives carrying two heavy boxes of library books. \"Could you hold the door, please?\" he asks. A kind student holds it open. Nobody checks who he is. All that technology was beaten by one polite request. That is **social engineering**.\n\n**Social engineering** means tricking people, rather than computers, into giving away access or information. It works on feelings such as kindness, trust, fear and curiosity.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what social engineering is and why it works.\n- Recognise five common methods: pretexting, baiting, phishing, tailgating and shoulder surfing.\n- Use four questions to pause and check any unusual request.\n- Respond safely when someone asks for a password or login code.\n\n## 💡 The Trojan Horse: An Old Trick\n\nAn old Greek legend tells of the city of Troy. Its walls held out against an army for ten years. Then the army left a huge wooden horse outside the gates as a \"gift\". The people of Troy proudly wheeled it inside. At night, soldiers hidden in the horse climbed out and opened the gates.\n\nThe walls never failed. The **people** were tricked into opening them. Modern tricksters use the same human feelings:\n\n- **Helpfulness and politeness:** \"Could you just do me a quick favour?\"\n- **Trust in authority:** \"This is the IT department.\"\n- **Fear and urgency:** \"Act now or your account will be deleted!\"\n- **Curiosity:** \"You will not believe what is in this file.\"\n\nNone of these feelings is bad. Kindness and trust are good qualities. The defence is simply to **pause and check** before acting.\n\n## 🎭 Five Common Methods\n\n### Pretexting (a made-up story)\nA **pretext** is a false reason given to hide the real one. The trickster invents a believable situation.\n\n> \"Hello, I'm Tariq from IT support. Someone just tried to log in to your account from another country. Please read me the six-digit code on your phone so I can lock them out.\"\n\nLook at the tools used: **authority** (\"IT support\"), **fear** (\"someone tried to log in\") and **urgency** (\"so I can lock them out\"). Real IT staff never need your code. The code is what *they* would need to get in.\n\n### Baiting (a tempting gift)\nA USB stick labelled \"Exam Answers 2026\" is left in the school car park. Curiosity tempts someone to plug it in. The stick may hold **malware**: software built to cause harm (Chapter 12 covers it). Never plug in a found USB device. Hand it to a teacher or the office.\n\n### Phishing, smishing and vishing (fake messages)\n- **Phishing:** a fake email that pretends to come from a school, a delivery company or a charity.\n- **Smishing:** the same trick by text message (SMS). For example: \"Your parcel is waiting. Update your address at this link.\"\n- **Vishing:** the same trick by voice call. Callers can fake the number shown on your screen. Some even use computer-made voices that sound like a real person.\n\nChapter 4 teaches you how to take a phishing message apart.\n\n### Tailgating (following someone through a door)\nA person without a pass follows closely behind someone who has one, often while carrying boxes so it feels rude to refuse. This is exactly what happened in our story.\n\n### Shoulder surfing (looking over your shoulder)\nSomeone on a bus or in the library quietly watches you type your PIN or password. Cover the keypad, angle your screen, and be aware of who is near you.\n\n## 🛡️ Defending the Human Firewall\n\nA **firewall** is a barrier that blocks unwanted traffic. Aware people act as a \"human firewall\".\n\nIslam teaches us to check news before acting on it (*Tabayyun*):\n\n> *\"O you who have believed, if there comes to you a disobedient one with information, investigate, lest you harm a people out of ignorance and become, over what you have done, regretful.\"* (Surah Al-Hujurat 49:6)\n\n### The four questions to ask before acting\n1. **Did I start this?** A message or call you did not expect deserves extra care.\n2. **Is someone rushing me?** \"Within 10 minutes or else\" is a warning sign.\n3. **Am I being asked for something secret?** A password, a login code, or a parent's card details.\n4. **Can I check another way?** Hang up and call back using the official number from the school website or a printed letter. This is called checking **out-of-band**: through a separate channel the trickster does not control.\n\n### Risky reply vs safe reply\n\n| Situation | ❌ Risky reply | ✅ Safe reply |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Caller asks for your login code | \"OK, it's 482 913.\" | \"I never share codes. I'll call the school office myself.\" |\n| Stranger asks you to hold the secure door | Holds it open | \"Sorry, please use your own card or sign in at reception.\" |\n| Message says \"Your account closes today\" | Clicks the link | Opens the site by typing its address, and checks there |\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Never share a password or a login code with anyone, including people claiming to be support staff.\n- When a message makes you feel rushed or scared, stop. That feeling is the trick.\n- Call back using a number you found yourself, not one the caller gave you.\n- Do not plug in USB sticks you find.\n- Tell a teacher or parent about strange requests. Reporting helps protect others too.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"I would never fall for that. Only careless people get tricked.\"\n\n**What happens:** tricksters target busy, tired or kind people at the worst moment, such as during exams or just before a deadline. Clever people are tricked every day because the message *feels* real.\n\n**Fixed thinking:** \"Anyone can be tricked, so I follow my checking routine every time.\" A habit protects you even when you are rushed.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nPractise the \"Pause and Check\" habit with a made-up text message:\n\n> \"School Library: your account will be closed in 24 hours. Confirm your password here: library-help.example.org\"\n\nWrite down which of the four questions raise a warning, and what you would do instead.\n\n*Hint:* who started the contact, is there a deadline, and what is being asked for?\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Social engineering tricks people instead of computers.\n- It uses good feelings, such as helpfulness and trust, and strong ones, such as fear and urgency.\n- Five common methods: pretexting, baiting, phishing (and smishing, vishing), tailgating and shoulder surfing.\n- Ask four questions: Did I start this? Am I rushed? Is a secret wanted? Can I check another way?\n- Nobody genuine ever needs your password or login code.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is \"tailgating\" in physical security?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nGetting into a secure place by following closely behind someone who is allowed in, for example through a card-access door.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nSomeone calls saying they are from \"IT Support\". They ask for the 2FA code that just arrived on your phone \"to fix a security problem\". Name two warning signs and say what you should do.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**Warning signs:** you did not start the contact; they claim authority; they create urgency; and they ask for a secret code.\n\n**What to do:** never share the code. End the call. Report it to the real IT department, using a phone number or email you look up yourself.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nMake a one-page \"Pause and Check\" poster for your class.\n\n1. Write the four checking questions in simple words.\n2. Add one short made-up example each of pretexting, baiting and smishing. Use made-up names and example.org addresses.\n3. Add one sentence telling students who to report to.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n**PAUSE AND CHECK**\n1. Did I start this?\n2. Am I being rushed?\n3. Are they asking for a secret?\n4. Can I check another way?\n\n- *Pretexting:* \"I'm Sara from the exam office. Tell me your password so I can upload your marks.\"\n- *Baiting:* a USB stick labelled \"Sports Day Photos\" left on a bench.\n- *Smishing:* \"Your library fine is unpaid. Pay at fines.example.org within 1 hour.\"\n\n**Report it:** tell your class teacher or the IT office straight away. It is never a nuisance.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-social-engineering-and-phishing/ch04-spotting-phishing-and-fraud.md": "# Chapter 4: Spotting Phishing, Spoofing & Online Fraud 🎣\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nMaryam's grandmother receives a letter that looks like it came from her bank. It has a logo, a stamp and a polite tone. But the return address is a small post box in another city. The letter also says she must \"reply today or lose her savings\". She shows it to Maryam before doing anything. Together they phone the bank using the number printed on her bank card. The bank says it never sent the letter. Checking the details, and checking another way, saved her.\n\n**Phishing** is the online version of that letter: a fake message made to look real, so that you give away passwords or money. **Spoofing** means faking who a message is from.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Take a phishing email apart and find its warning signs.\n- Read a web address correctly and spot lookalike domains.\n- Check where a link really goes before clicking it.\n- Explain in simple words what SPF, DKIM and DMARC do, and what they cannot do.\n\n## 🔍 Anatomy of a Phishing Email\n\nHere is a made-up phishing email. Each arrow marks a warning sign, or **red flag**.\n\n```\nFrom: \"Junior Coders Academy\"\n  <alerts@juni0r-coders.example.net>\n      ◄── Flag 1: lookalike address\nSubject: URGENT: verify within 12 hours!\n      ◄── Flag 2: rushing you\n\nDear Student,\nWe noticed strange logins on your\naccount. Verify your details:\n\n[ Secure My Account ]\n      ◄── Flag 3: hidden destination\n(Real link: http://198.51.100.88/login)\n\nFailure to verify will delete\nall your files.\n      ◄── Flag 4: a threat\n\nSecurity Team\n      ◄── Flag 5: no real name\n```\n\nNotice the zero in `juni0r`. Notice the link goes to a bare number address (an **IP address**) instead of a named site. No real school deletes your work for missing a 12-hour deadline.\n\n## 🚩 Six Red Flags\n\n### Lookalike domains\nA **domain** is the name part of a web or email address, such as `example.org`. Tricksters register names that look almost the same as a real one. This is called **typosquatting**.\n\n| Real | Fake lookalikes |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `library.example.org` | `library.examp1e.org` (digit 1 for letter l) |\n| | `library-example.org` (a dash makes a different domain) |\n| | `example.org.login-check.example.net` |\n\n**How to read an address:** find the first single `/` after `https://`. Look just to the left of it. The last two parts there usually name the real owner. In the third fake, the owner is `example.net`, not `example.org`. Everything to the left of that is just decoration.\n\n### Letters from other alphabets\nSome letters in other alphabets look the same as English ones. The Cyrillic letter `а` looks just like the English `a`. So `exаmple.org` written with a Cyrillic `а` looks identical to `example.org`. Browsers turn such names into a special form called **Punycode** so they can be spotted:\n\n```\nexаmple.org (with Cyrillic а)\n  ──►  xn--exmple-4nf.org\n```\n\nIf you see `xn--` in an address bar, look carefully.\n\n### Links that hide their destination\nIn HTML, the text you see on a link and the place it goes can be different. The `href` part is the real destination:\n\n```html\n<!-- The visible text says one thing... -->\n<a href=\"https://login.examp1e.net/\">\n  https://library.example.org/login\n</a>\n<!-- ...but the href goes somewhere else -->\n```\n\n**Defence:** on a computer, **hover** your mouse over a link. The real address appears at the bottom of the window. On a phone, press and hold the link to preview it. Better still, do not use the link at all: type the address you already know.\n\n### Generic greetings\n\"Dear Customer\" or \"Dear User\" instead of your name. Real services that know you often use your name, though a tidy greeting alone proves nothing.\n\n### Dangerous attachments\nBe very careful with files ending in `.exe`, `.scr` or `.vbs`. These are programs, not documents. Watch for double endings such as `timetable.pdf.exe`. Windows hides known file endings by default, so it may show only `timetable.pdf`. Turn on **File name extensions** in File Explorer's View menu to see the full name.\n\n### Pressure, threats and too-good offers\n\"Act now\", \"your account will be deleted\", or \"you have won a free tablet\". Strong feelings switch off careful thinking. That is exactly why tricksters use them.\n\n## 📬 SPF, DKIM and DMARC\n\nEmail was invented without any way to prove who sent a message. Three add-ons, published in a domain's settings, help mail servers check:\n\n- **SPF** (Sender Policy Framework): a list of the servers allowed to send email for a domain.\n- **DKIM** (DomainKeys Identified Mail): a digital signature on each email. It proves the message came from that domain and was not changed on the way.\n- **DMARC**: tells receiving servers what to do when SPF or DKIM checks fail: let it through, put it in spam, or reject it. It also sends reports to the domain owner.\n\n> ⚠️ **What they cannot do:** these checks prove an email really came from `juni0r-coders.example.net`. They cannot tell you that this domain is a fake. A trickster can set up perfect SPF and DKIM for their own lookalike domain. Your eyes are still needed.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Read the sender's full address, not just the display name.\n- Read the domain from the right, just before the first single `/`.\n- Hover or press-and-hold before you tap any link.\n- When in doubt, open the website yourself by typing its address or using a bookmark.\n- Never open unexpected attachments, even from a friend whose account may have been taken over.\n- Report phishing using your email app's \"Report phishing\" button, then delete it.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"The email has the right logo and a padlock link, so it is genuine.\"\n\n**What happens:** logos are easy to copy. The padlock (HTTPS) only means the connection is encrypted. It says nothing about who owns the site. A fake site can have a padlock too.\n\n**Fixed thinking:** check the **domain**, not the decoration. Then check another way, such as the official app or website you open yourself.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen the **Phishing** tab in the Cybersecurity Lab Studio.\n\n1. Read the sample email.\n2. Find the lookalike sender, the hidden link and the pressure words.\n3. Write down which flag was easiest to miss.\n\n*Hint:* look at every character of the sender's domain, slowly.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Phishing is a fake message designed to steal passwords or money.\n- Red flags: lookalike domains, hidden link destinations, generic greetings, risky attachments, pressure and threats.\n- Read a domain from the right, before the first single `/`.\n- SPF, DKIM and DMARC help servers check senders, but a lookalike domain can still pass them.\n- The padlock means \"encrypted\", not \"trustworthy\".\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nHow can you find out where a link in an email really goes before clicking it?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nOn a computer, hover the mouse over the link and read the address shown at the bottom of the window. On a phone, press and hold the link to see a preview. The safest choice is to type the known address yourself.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWhich domain really owns each address?\n\n1. `https://example.org/books`\n2. `https://example.org.renew-card.example.net/login`\n3. `https://books.example.org/renew`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `example.org`\n2. `example.net`: everything before it is decoration, so this is a trap.\n3. `example.org`: `books` is just a section (a **subdomain**) of the real site.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWrite your own made-up phishing email for a class lesson, then a \"teacher's answer key\".\n\n1. Use only made-up names and `example.org` or `example.net` addresses.\n2. Include at least four red flags.\n3. In the answer key, list each flag and the safe action a student should take.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```\nFrom: \"City Library\"\n  <notice@citylibrary-help.example.net>\nSubject: FINAL WARNING: card ends tonight\n\nDear Member,\nYour library card has an unpaid fine.\nPay within 2 hours to keep borrowing:\n[ Pay Now ]\n(real link: http://203.0.113.5/pay)\nAttachment: fine_notice.pdf.exe\n```\n\n**Answer key**\n1. Sender domain is `example.net`, not the library's real domain: check the address.\n2. \"Within 2 hours\" and \"final warning\": pressure. Slow down.\n3. The link goes to a bare IP address: do not click; visit the library site yourself.\n4. `fine_notice.pdf.exe` is a program pretending to be a PDF: never open it.\n5. \"Dear Member\": no name.\n\n**Safe action:** report the email as phishing and ask the library directly.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-web-application-vulnerabilities/ch05-owasp-top-10-overview.md": "# Chapter 5: The OWASP Top 10 — Defending the Web 🌐\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBefore a school opens each morning, the caretaker uses a printed checklist. Are the fire doors closed? Are the gas taps off in the science lab? Is the first-aid box full? He did not invent this list himself. It was written by safety experts who studied many real accidents. Following it means he does not have to remember everything alone. Web developers have a similar checklist, written by experts who studied many real website problems.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what OWASP is and what the **OWASP Top 10** list is for.\n- Name the ten risk areas in the 2025 list in simple words.\n- Apply the rule \"never trust user input\" to a form.\n- Tell apart **validation**, **escaping** and **parameterization**.\n\n## 🗺️ What Is OWASP?\n\n**OWASP** (the Open Worldwide Application Security Project) is a non-profit community. Its members share free guides and tools about building safe software.\n\nEvery few years, OWASP studies data from many real web applications. It then publishes the **OWASP Top 10**: ten of the most important risk areas for websites. The newest edition is the **2025** list. You will also see the older 2021 list quoted online; most of the ideas are the same.\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ A01 Broken Access Control                │\n│ A02 Security Misconfiguration            │\n│ A03 Software Supply Chain Failures       │\n│ A04 Cryptographic Failures               │\n│ A05 Injection                            │\n│ A06 Insecure Design                      │\n│ A07 Authentication Failures              │\n│ A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures  │\n│ A09 Security Logging & Alerting Failures │\n│ A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions│\n└──────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## 📖 The Ten Risks in Plain Words\n\n| Code | Plain meaning | School-portal example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| A01 Broken Access Control | Users can reach things they should not | A student opens another student's report by changing a number in the address (Chapter 8) |\n| A02 Security Misconfiguration | Unsafe settings | Error pages show the database password; a default admin password is never changed |\n| A03 Software Supply Chain Failures | Problems in code you did not write yourself | An add-on library has a known hole, or a fake package is installed |\n| A04 Cryptographic Failures | Secrets not protected properly | Passwords stored as plain text; no HTTPS (Chapters 2, 10, 11) |\n| A05 Injection | User text treated as commands | Typed text changes an SQL query or adds a script to a page (Chapters 6, 7) |\n| A06 Insecure Design | The plan itself is unsafe | \"Forgot password\" asks only for a birthday, which classmates know |\n| A07 Authentication Failures | Weak logins | No limit on password guesses; no 2FA for admins (Chapter 2) |\n| A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures | Trusting things that could be tampered with | Installing an update without checking it really came from the maker |\n| A09 Security Logging & Alerting Failures | Nobody notices an attack | No record of failed logins, so a break-in goes unseen (Chapter 14) |\n| A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions | Errors handled badly | When the database is busy, the login check crashes and lets people in |\n\nWords to know: a **library** (in coding) is ready-made code you add to your project. An **exception** is an error the program has to deal with while running.\n\n## 🧱 The Core Rule: Never Trust User Input\n\nIf you remember one rule from this part of the book, make it this one:\n\n> ⚠️ **All data from outside your program is untrusted until you have checked it.**\n\n\"Outside\" includes much more than form boxes:\n\n- Form fields (`<input>`, `<textarea>`)\n- Values in the address bar, such as `?id=42&search=books`\n- **Cookies** (small pieces of data a site stores in your browser)\n- Uploaded files, such as `photo.jpg`\n- Data sent from other websites and services\n\nRemember: anyone can change what their own browser sends. Hidden form fields and drop-down menus can be edited too. Checks in JavaScript help the user, but only checks on the **server** protect the data.\n\n## 🛡️ Three Shields Against Bad Input\n\n### Shield 1: Validation (is it the right shape?)\n**Validation** means checking that data looks the way you expect, *before* you use it. Is the age really a whole number between 5 and 19?\n\n```php\n<?php\n$inputs = ['14', '250', 'fourteen', '-3'];\nforeach ($inputs as $age) {\n    $ok = filter_var($age, FILTER_VALIDATE_INT,\n        ['options' => ['min_range' => 5, 'max_range' => 19]]);\n    echo $age, ' => ', $ok === false ? 'rejected' : 'accepted', \"\\n\";\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n14 => accepted\n250 => rejected\nfourteen => rejected\n-3 => rejected\n```\n\nIt is best to **allow** only what you expect (an \"allow list\"), rather than trying to list every bad thing.\n\n### Shield 2: Escaping (show it as plain text)\n**Escaping** means changing special characters so a browser shows them as text instead of reading them as code. In PHP, `htmlspecialchars()` does this:\n\n```php\n<?php\necho htmlspecialchars('<b>Great book!</b>');\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n&lt;b&gt;Great book!&lt;/b&gt;\n```\n\nThe browser now *shows* the tags as text instead of making the words bold. Chapter 6 uses this to stop script attacks.\n\n### Shield 3: Parameterization (keep data apart from commands)\n**Parameterization** means sending the command and the user's data to the database **separately**. The data can never become part of the command. These are called **prepared statements**. Chapter 7 covers them in detail.\n\n| Shield | Question it asks | Used when |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Validation | \"Is this the right shape?\" | Data arrives |\n| Escaping | \"Will this show as text only?\" | Data is shown on a page |\n| Parameterization | \"Is data kept apart from commands?\" | Data goes into a database query |\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\nWhen you build any form:\n\n- List every place where data enters your program.\n- Validate each value on the server: type, length and allowed range.\n- Escape every value you print on a page.\n- Use prepared statements for every database query.\n- Show friendly error messages to users, and keep the detailed errors in a private log.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"My form uses `<input type=\"number\" min=\"5\" max=\"19\">`, so the age is always safe.\"\n\n**What happens:** those HTML rules only guide honest users in the browser. Anyone can change the page or send a request directly, and your server receives `\"fourteen\"` or `\"250\"` anyway.\n\n**Fixed version:** keep the HTML rules for a friendly form, **and** validate again on the server, as in the `filter_var()` example above.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nTake any form you have built in the HTML or PHP tracks, such as a book-review or contact form.\n\n1. List every field.\n2. For each, write the rule it must follow (for example: \"name: 1–50 letters\").\n3. Mark where you would escape it when showing it on a page.\n\n*Hint:* hidden fields and the address bar count as input too.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- OWASP is a non-profit community; its Top 10 lists the most important website risk areas.\n- In the 2025 list, Broken Access Control is still number one.\n- Never trust user input: check it on the server, every time.\n- Validation checks the shape, escaping makes text safe to show, parameterization keeps data out of commands.\n- Browser checks are for convenience; server checks are for security.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is number one (A01) in the OWASP Top 10 for 2025, and what does it mean?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**Broken Access Control**: users can see or change things they are not allowed to, such as another person's records.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nFor each case, choose the shield that fits best: validation, escaping or parameterization.\n\n1. A comment is shown on the class news page.\n2. A \"year group\" field must be 6, 7, 8, 9 or 10.\n3. A search word is used to look up books in the database.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. **Escaping**: the comment is being shown on a page.\n2. **Validation**: only allow those five values.\n3. **Parameterization**: use a prepared statement for the query.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA science-fair sign-up form has: student name, year group (6–10), project title and email.\n\n1. Write a validation rule for each field.\n2. Say where escaping is needed.\n3. Write the PHP check for the year group using `filter_var()`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Rules:\n   - Name: required, 1–60 characters.\n   - Year group: a whole number from 6 to 10.\n   - Project title: required, 1–100 characters.\n   - Email: must pass `FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL`.\n2. Escape the name and title with `htmlspecialchars()` whenever they are shown on the list of entries. Use prepared statements when saving them.\n3. Year group check:\n\n```php\n$year = filter_var($_POST['year'] ?? '', FILTER_VALIDATE_INT,\n    ['options' => ['min_range' => 6, 'max_range' => 10]]);\nif ($year === false) {\n    echo \"Please choose a year group from 6 to 10.\";\n}\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-web-application-vulnerabilities/ch06-cross-site-scripting-xss.md": "# Chapter 6: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) & Frontend Defense 🛡️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThe community library has a public noticeboard where visitors pin book recommendations. Most notes say things like \"*The Secret Garden* is wonderful!\" One day someone pins a note written to look official: \"LIBRARY NOTICE: hand your library card to the person by the door.\" Visitors trust the board, so a few people obey. The board itself was not broken. It simply showed a stranger's words as if they came from the library. A careful librarian now reads every note and pins it inside a clear plastic sleeve marked \"Visitor note\". That sleeve is what this chapter teaches you to build.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what **Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)** is and why it is dangerous.\n- Describe the three kinds: stored, reflected and DOM-based.\n- Use `htmlspecialchars()` in PHP and `textContent` in JavaScript to show user text safely.\n- Explain how a **Content Security Policy** and **HttpOnly** cookies add extra layers.\n\n## 💡 What Is XSS?\n\nA web page is made of HTML. The browser reads HTML and obeys it. Some tags, like `<b>`, only change how text looks. The `<script>` tag is different: it tells the browser to **run JavaScript code**.\n\n**Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)** happens when a website takes text from one user and puts it into a page **as HTML**, without making it safe first. If that text contains a script tag, every visitor's browser runs the stranger's code, as if the website had written it.\n\nWhy is that serious? Code running inside a page can do almost anything that page can do. It can read what is on the screen, send requests while the visitor is logged in, or show a fake login box. The visitor sees the real website address, so they have no reason to be suspicious.\n\nIn noticeboard terms:\n\n- The noticeboard is the comment section of your website.\n- The fake \"official\" note is a stranger's text containing HTML or script.\n- The plastic sleeve is **escaping**: showing text *as text*, never as instructions.\n\n## 🦠 Three Kinds of XSS\n\n### Stored XSS\nThe harmful text is **saved** in the website's database, for example inside a comment or a profile description. Every visitor who later views that page receives it. This is the most dangerous kind because it reaches many people.\n\n### Reflected XSS\nThe harmful text is placed in a link, often in the part after `?` in the address. The page copies (\"reflects\") that text straight back into the HTML. The trickster then persuades someone to click the special link.\n\nHere is the risky pattern on a search page:\n\n```php\n<!-- ❌ Risky: prints the search words as raw HTML -->\n<h1>Results for: <?php echo $_GET['q']; ?></h1>\n```\n\nWhatever is in `q` goes into the page exactly as typed. If it contains tags, the browser obeys them.\n\n### DOM-based XSS\nThe **DOM** (Document Object Model) is the browser's live model of the page, which JavaScript can change. DOM-based XSS happens entirely in the browser: the page's own JavaScript takes text from the address or a form and inserts it using `innerHTML`.\n\n## 🛡️ Defence 1: Escape Output (the Plastic Sleeve)\n\n**Escaping** (also called output encoding) replaces the special characters of HTML with safe codes called **HTML entities**. The browser then shows them as ordinary characters.\n\n| Character | HTML entity |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `<` | `&lt;` |\n| `>` | `&gt;` |\n| `&` | `&amp;` |\n| `\"` | `&quot;` |\n| `'` | `&#039;` |\n\n### In PHP\n```php\n<?php\n$comment = \"Tom's <i>book</i>\";\necho htmlspecialchars($comment, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nTom&#039;s &lt;i&gt;book&lt;/i&gt;\n```\n\nIn the browser this displays as `Tom's <i>book</i>`: the tags appear as plain text and do nothing. A script tag would be shown in exactly the same harmless way.\n\nThe fixed search page:\n\n```php\n<!-- ✅ Safe: the search words are always shown as text -->\n<h1>Results for: <?php echo htmlspecialchars($_GET['q'] ?? '', ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8'); ?></h1>\n```\n\n### In JavaScript\n```javascript\nconst userComment = \"<b>Great</b> book\";\n\n// ❌ Risky: the browser reads it as HTML\ncommentDiv.innerHTML = userComment;\n\n// ✅ Safe: the browser shows it as plain text\ncommentDiv.textContent = userComment;\n```\n\nWhat the reader sees:\n\n- With `innerHTML`: **Great** book (the tag was obeyed, so it could also have been a script).\n- With `textContent`: `<b>Great</b> book` (the exact characters, with no effect).\n\n> 💡 **Rule:** escape when you *show* data, not only when you save it. The same text might be shown in many places.\n\n## 🛡️ Defence 2: Content Security Policy (CSP)\n\nA **Content Security Policy** is an instruction the server sends to the browser in an **HTTP header** (extra information sent along with a page). It says which scripts are allowed to run:\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'\n```\n\n`'self'` means \"only scripts from files on this same website\". Scripts written directly inside the page's HTML are blocked. So even if a harmful tag slips into a page, the browser refuses to run it.\n\nCSP is a **second layer**, not a replacement for escaping. Defense in depth again!\n\n## 🛡️ Defence 3: HttpOnly Cookies\n\nA **session cookie** is the small value that keeps you logged in. Adding the `HttpOnly` flag means JavaScript cannot read that cookie at all:\n\n```http\nSet-Cookie: session_id=abc987; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\nIf an XSS bug ever slips through, the stranger's code still cannot copy your login cookie.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Escape every piece of user text you print with `htmlspecialchars($x, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8')`.\n- In JavaScript, use `textContent` for user text. Avoid `innerHTML` unless the HTML is your own.\n- Never build HTML by joining strings that contain user input.\n- Send a Content Security Policy header on your sites.\n- Mark session cookies `HttpOnly` and `Secure`.\n- Test only on your own pages or the lab studio.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"I removed the word `<script>` from comments, so my site is safe.\"\n\n**What happens:** HTML has many other ways to run code, such as event attributes on ordinary tags. Blocking one word is like locking one window and leaving the others open.\n\n**Fixed version:** do not try to list every bad thing. Escape *everything* on output with `htmlspecialchars()` or `textContent`, so no user text is ever read as HTML.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen the **XSS** tab in the Cybersecurity Lab Studio.\n\n1. Type `<b>Hello</b>` into the box.\n2. Compare the **Vulnerable Output (innerHTML)** panel with the **Secure Output** panel.\n3. Write one sentence explaining the difference.\n\n*Hint:* one panel *obeys* the tag; the other *shows* it.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- XSS happens when user text is put into a page as HTML instead of as text.\n- Stored XSS is saved and reaches every visitor; reflected XSS comes from a link; DOM-based XSS happens in the browser's own JavaScript.\n- The main fix is escaping output: `htmlspecialchars()` in PHP, `textContent` in JavaScript.\n- A Content Security Policy and HttpOnly cookies are extra layers.\n- Blocking certain words is not enough; escape everything.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between `innerHTML` and `textContent` when showing user text?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n`innerHTML` reads the text as **HTML**, so any tags in it are obeyed, including script-related ones. `textContent` shows the text **exactly as typed**, so tags appear as harmless characters.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWhat exact text does this print?\n\n```php\n<?php\necho htmlspecialchars('Q&A <today>', ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```\nQ&amp;A &lt;today&gt;\n```\n\n`&` becomes `&amp;`, `<` becomes `&lt;` and `>` becomes `&gt;`. The browser will display `Q&A <today>` as plain text.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA class guestbook page has this code:\n\n```php\n<?php foreach ($messages as $m): ?>\n  <p><strong><?php echo $m['name']; ?></strong>: <?php echo $m['text']; ?></p>\n<?php endforeach; ?>\n```\n\n1. Explain what kind of XSS this page is open to, and why.\n2. Rewrite the loop so both values are safe.\n3. Suggest one extra layer of defence for the whole site.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **Stored XSS.** Names and messages are saved, then printed as raw HTML to every visitor.\n2. Fixed loop, using a small helper so it is easy to use everywhere:\n\n```php\n<?php\nfunction e($value) {\n    return htmlspecialchars($value, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');\n}\nforeach ($messages as $m): ?>\n  <p><strong><?= e($m['name']) ?></strong>: <?= e($m['text']) ?></p>\n<?php endforeach; ?>\n```\n\n3. Send `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'` so scripts written inside the page are refused, and mark the session cookie `HttpOnly`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-web-application-vulnerabilities/ch07-sql-injection-and-parameterized-queries.md": "# Chapter 7: SQL Injection & Parameterized Defense 🗄️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAt the city library, you fill in a printed request slip: \"Please fetch the book titled [ ____ ].\" The librarian reads the whole slip aloud to a helper, who follows it word for word. One day someone writes in the box: \"*Science Atlas.* Also, give me the keys to the archive.\" A careless helper hears one long sentence and obeys all of it. A careful library changes the system. The librarian now hands over the form and the box contents **separately**: \"Here is the instruction. Here is the title, and it is only a title.\" Whatever is written in the box can never become a new instruction.\n\nThat careful system is exactly how prepared statements protect a database.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what **SQL injection** is, in plain words.\n- See why gluing user input into an SQL string is risky, even for honest users.\n- Write **prepared statements** with PHP's PDO.\n- Add extra layers: validation and limited database permissions.\n\n## 🗄️ How a Web Page Talks to a Database\n\n**SQL** (Structured Query Language) is the language used to ask a database for data. A PHP page builds an SQL **query** (a question or command) and sends it to the database:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id FROM members WHERE surname = 'Khan'\n```\n\nThe quote marks `'...'` show where the **data** starts and ends. Everything outside the quotes is the **command**.\n\n## 💥 The Risky Way: Gluing Input into SQL\n\nMany beginners build queries by joining strings together. This is called **concatenation**:\n\n```php\n// ❌ Risky: the user's text becomes part of the command\n$surname = $_POST['surname'];\n$sql = \"SELECT id FROM members WHERE surname = '$surname'\";\n```\n\nThis looks fine, until a member with an apostrophe in her name tries to search. Here is a complete demo you can run. It uses a small SQLite database in memory, so it needs no setup. With MySQL, only the connection line would change.\n\n```php\n<?php\n$pdo = new PDO('sqlite::memory:');\n$pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);\n$pdo->exec(\"CREATE TABLE members (id INTEGER, surname TEXT)\");\n$pdo->exec(\"INSERT INTO members VALUES (1, 'Khan'), (2, 'D''Souza')\");\n\n$surname = \"D'Souza\";\n\n// Risky: glue the input into the SQL text\n$sql = \"SELECT id FROM members WHERE surname = '$surname'\";\necho $sql, \"\\n\";\ntry {\n    $pdo->query($sql);\n} catch (PDOException $e) {\n    echo \"Error!\\n\";\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nSELECT id FROM members WHERE surname = 'D'Souza'\nError!\n```\n\n### What went wrong?\nThe apostrophe in `D'Souza` looks exactly like the quote mark that ends the data. So the database thinks the name is just `D`, and then finds the leftover word `Souza'` sitting where commands belong. It cannot understand it, so it fails.\n\nHere an honest user only caused an error. But think about what this means. **The user's typing was able to change where the data ended and the command began.** Someone with bad intentions could type text that closes the data early and then adds command words of their own. The database would obey them as if the programmer had written them. They might read other people's records, skip the password check, or delete data.\n\nThat is **SQL injection**: user input \"injected\" into a command, changing what the command does. It belongs to **A05 Injection** in the OWASP Top 10 (Chapter 5).\n\n> 🔒 We will not show attack inputs here. You only need to understand the cause: **data and commands were mixed in one string.** Fix the cause and every variation of the attack fails.\n\n## 🛡️ The Fix: Prepared Statements\n\nA **prepared statement** sends the database the command and the data **separately**, like the careful librarian.\n\n1. First, PHP sends the query with a placeholder `?` where the data will go.\n2. The database reads and fixes the shape of the command.\n3. Then PHP sends the user's data. The database treats it **only** as a value, never as a command.\n\nPHP's **PDO** (PHP Data Objects) is the built-in tool for talking to databases. Continuing the demo:\n\n```php\n// ✅ Safe: prepared statement\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT id, surname FROM members WHERE surname = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$surname]);\nprint_r($stmt->fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nArray\n(\n    [id] => 2\n    [surname] => D'Souza\n)\n```\n\nThe apostrophe is no problem now. The database looks for a member whose surname is exactly the characters `D'Souza`, and finds her.\n\n### Why this stops injection\nWhatever the user types, it is only ever **the value being searched for**. If someone types command words into the box, the database simply looks for a member whose name is literally those strange words. It finds no such member and returns no rows. The command itself cannot change.\n\nYou can also use named placeholders, which are easier to read when there are several values:\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\n    \"SELECT title FROM loans WHERE member_id = :member AND returned = :done\"\n);\n$stmt->execute([':member' => $memberId, ':done' => 0]);\n```\n\n### A safe login check\nPrepared statements go together with the password hashing from Chapter 2:\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT id, password_hash FROM users WHERE username = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$_POST['username']]);\n$user = $stmt->fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n\nif ($user && password_verify($_POST['password'], $user['password_hash'])) {\n    echo \"Welcome!\";\n} else {\n    echo \"Wrong username or password.\";\n}\n```\n\nNotice the error message does not say *which* one was wrong. That gives a guesser less information.\n\n## 🧱 Extra Layers (Defense in Depth)\n\nPrepared statements are the main fix. Add these layers as well:\n\n- **Validate input** (Chapter 5): an ID should be a whole number; a year group should be 6–10.\n- **Least privilege:** the database account your website uses should have only the permissions it needs. A library site may need to read and add loans, but it never needs to delete whole tables.\n- **Hide error details:** show users a friendly message and keep the technical error in a private log.\n\n| Layer | What it does |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Prepared statements | Data can never become a command |\n| Validation | Strange values are rejected early |\n| Least privilege | Even a mistake cannot delete everything |\n| Hidden errors | Visitors learn nothing about your database |\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Search your PHP files for queries that contain `$` variables inside the SQL string. Rewrite each one with `?` placeholders.\n- Use `prepare()` and `execute()` for every query that includes any outside value.\n- Give your website's database user only the permissions it needs.\n- Test with names that contain apostrophes, such as `D'Souza`. Your form should handle them perfectly.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** using `prepare()`, but still gluing the variable into the string:\n\n```php\n// ❌ Still risky: the data is inside the SQL text\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM members WHERE surname = '$surname'\");\n$stmt->execute();\n```\n\n**What happens:** `prepare()` only protects values that are passed **separately**. Here the input is already part of the command text, so nothing has changed.\n\n**Fixed version:**\n\n```php\n// ✅ Placeholder in the SQL, value passed to execute()\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM members WHERE surname = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$surname]);\n```\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen the **SQLi** tab in the Cybersecurity Lab Studio.\n\n1. Type an ordinary surname, then a surname with an apostrophe.\n2. Compare the **Vulnerable Concatenation Query** panel with the **Secure Prepared Statement** panel.\n3. Notice how the risky panel's command changes shape, while the safe panel's command never changes.\n\n*Hint:* look at where each quote mark ends up.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- SQL injection happens when user input is glued into an SQL command and changes what it does.\n- Even an honest apostrophe can break a concatenated query; that is the warning sign.\n- Prepared statements send the command and the data separately, so data can never become a command.\n- Use `?` or `:name` placeholders and pass values to `execute()`.\n- Add validation, least privilege and hidden error details as extra layers.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the best defence against SQL injection, and what does it keep apart?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**Prepared statements** (also called parameterized queries). They keep the **command** apart from the **data**, so user input is only ever treated as a value.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nRewrite this risky code as a prepared statement:\n\n```php\n$sql = \"SELECT title FROM books WHERE author = '\" . $_GET['author'] . \"'\";\n$result = $pdo->query($sql);\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT title FROM books WHERE author = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$_GET['author'] ?? '']);\n$result = $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n```\n\nThe placeholder `?` marks where the data goes; the value is passed separately to `execute()`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA charity's \"find my donation receipt\" page has this code:\n\n```php\n$id = $_GET['receipt'];\n$sql = \"SELECT * FROM receipts WHERE id = $id\";\n$rows = $pdo->query($sql)->fetchAll();\n```\n\n1. Explain in your own words why this is unsafe.\n2. Add validation so only whole numbers are accepted.\n3. Rewrite the query as a prepared statement.\n4. Name one more layer from this chapter that would help.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The visitor's text becomes part of the SQL command, so they could change what the command does.\n2. and 3:\n\n```php\n$id = filter_var($_GET['receipt'] ?? '', FILTER_VALIDATE_INT,\n    ['options' => ['min_range' => 1]]);\nif ($id === false) {\n    exit(\"Please enter a valid receipt number.\");\n}\n\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM receipts WHERE id = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$id]);\n$rows = $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n```\n\n4. **Least privilege**: give the website's database user read-only access to receipts. Also, check that the receipt belongs to the logged-in donor (Chapter 8 explains this access check).\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-web-application-vulnerabilities/ch08-csrf-and-broken-access-control.md": "# Chapter 8: CSRF & Broken Access Control 🚪\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAt Zainab's school, the office accepts signed notes from parents. One day a note arrives: \"Please let Zainab leave at 11 a.m.\" The signature looks right, but Zainab's mother never wrote it. A careful office now staples a **numbered slip** to every form it sends home. Only forms with the matching slip are accepted. The office also checks that the parent signing is really **this** child's parent, not just any parent. These two checks are the two halves of this chapter.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain **Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)** in plain words.\n- Protect forms with a secret **CSRF token** and **SameSite** cookies.\n- Explain **Broken Access Control** and **IDOR**.\n- Write an ownership check so users only see their own records.\n\n## 💡 How a Logged-In Browser Works\n\nWhen you log in to a website, it gives your browser a **session cookie**: a small value meaning \"this visitor is logged in as Zainab\". Your browser sends that cookie back automatically with every request to the same site. That is why you do not have to log in on every page.\n\nAutomatic is convenient. But it creates a gap: the site sees the cookie and assumes **you** chose to send the request.\n\n## 🎭 CSRF: The Forged Note\n\n**Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)** means another website tricks your browser into sending a request to a site where you are logged in. \"Cross-site\" means it comes from a different site. \"Forgery\" means it pretends to be you.\n\n```\nTab 1: logged in to school portal\nTab 2: you open an unknown page\n        │\n        │ the page quietly makes your\n        │ browser send a request to the\n        ▼ school portal\n [ portal sees YOUR cookie ]\n        │\n        ▼\n \"Change email\" runs as if you\n  asked for it\n```\n\nYou clicked nothing on the portal, yet an action happened in your name. The attacker never learned your password. They simply borrowed your browser's automatic cookie.\n\n### Which actions are at risk?\nAnything that **changes** something: changing an email address, deleting a project, submitting a vote in a class poll, posting a message. Pages that only show data are much less at risk. That is one reason why actions that change data must always use **POST** forms, never plain links (GET).\n\n## 🛡️ Defence 1: A Secret Form Token\n\nA **CSRF token** is a long random value the server creates and keeps in the user's session. The server also puts it in a hidden field in every form it shows. When the form comes back, the server checks the two match.\n\nAnother website cannot read your portal's pages, because browsers keep each site's pages private from other sites. This rule is called the **Same-Origin Policy**. So the other site cannot know the token, and its forged request fails.\n\n**Step 1: make the token** (once per session):\n\n```php\n<?php\nsession_start();\nif (empty($_SESSION['csrf_token'])) {\n    $_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));\n}\n```\n\n`random_bytes(32)` makes 32 truly random bytes. `bin2hex()` turns them into 64 readable characters.\n\n**Step 2: put it in the form:**\n\n```php\n<form action=\"change-email.php\" method=\"POST\">\n  <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"csrf_token\"\n         value=\"<?= htmlspecialchars($_SESSION['csrf_token']) ?>\">\n  <input type=\"email\" name=\"email\" required>\n  <button type=\"submit\">Save</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n**Step 3: check it** when the form arrives:\n\n```php\n<?php\nsession_start();\n$sent = $_POST['csrf_token'] ?? '';\nif (!hash_equals($_SESSION['csrf_token'] ?? '', $sent)) {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('This form has expired. Please reload the page and try again.');\n}\n// ...safe to change the email now\n```\n\n`hash_equals()` compares two strings safely. Here is a quick test:\n\n```php\n<?php\n$token = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));\necho strlen($token), \" characters\\n\";\nvar_dump(hash_equals($token, $token));\nvar_dump(hash_equals($token, 'guess'));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n64 characters\nbool(true)\nbool(false)\n```\n\n## 🛡️ Defence 2: SameSite Cookies\n\nThe `SameSite` setting tells the browser when to send a cookie with requests that start on **other** sites:\n\n```http\nSet-Cookie: session_id=abc987; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\n| Setting | What the browser does |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `Strict` | Never sends the cookie with requests that start on other sites |\n| `Lax` | Sends it when you follow a normal link to the site; not with POST forms or background requests from other sites |\n| `None` | Always sends it (must be `Secure`); avoid for login cookies |\n\nModern browsers treat cookies as `Lax` when no setting is given, but set it yourself. Use **both** tokens and SameSite: two layers are better than one.\n\n## 🚪 Broken Access Control\n\n**Access control** means deciding who may see or change each thing. **Broken Access Control** is when the website forgets to check. It is number one (A01) in the OWASP Top 10 (Chapter 5).\n\n### IDOR: changing a number in the address\n**IDOR** stands for **Insecure Direct Object Reference**. It means the site uses an ID from the address, but never checks that you are allowed to use that ID.\n\n```\nhttps://school-portal.example.org/report?id=1042\n```\n\nAisha is student 1042. If she changes the address to `id=1043` and sees Bilal's report, the site has an IDOR flaw. Being logged in is not enough. The site must check that **this** report belongs to **this** user.\n\n```php\n// ❌ Risky: checks nothing about ownership\n// (and glues input into SQL, too!)\n$report = $pdo->query(\"SELECT * FROM reports WHERE id = \" . $_GET['id']);\n```\n\n```php\n// ✅ Safe: the report must belong to the logged-in student\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\n    \"SELECT * FROM reports WHERE id = ? AND student_id = ?\"\n);\n$stmt->execute([$_GET['id'] ?? 0, $_SESSION['user_id']]);\n$report = $stmt->fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n\nif (!$report) {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('You do not have permission to view this report.');\n}\n```\n\nIf the report exists but belongs to someone else, the query returns nothing, and the visitor sees \"no permission\".\n\n### Other access-control checks\n- **Roles:** only teachers may edit marks. Check the role on the **server** for every action, not just by hiding the button.\n- **Deny by default:** if you are not sure a user is allowed, say no.\n- **Check every request:** a hidden page is not a protected page. Anyone can type its address.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Use POST forms for every action that changes data.\n- Add a CSRF token to every POST form and check it with `hash_equals()`.\n- Set session cookies to `Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` (or `Strict`).\n- In every query for a user's own data, add `AND owner_id = ?` with the logged-in user's ID.\n- Check roles on the server for every action.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"Only teachers see the *Edit marks* button, so students cannot edit marks.\"\n\n**What happens:** hiding a button does not protect anything. A student can still send the same request directly, for example by copying the form's address. If the server does not check the role, the change goes through.\n\n**Fixed version:** on the server, before saving:\n\n```php\nif (($_SESSION['role'] ?? '') !== 'teacher') {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('Only teachers can edit marks.');\n}\n```\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nLook at a PHP project of your own that has a login.\n\n1. Find every page that changes data. Does each one use POST and check a CSRF token?\n2. Find every page that reads `?id=` from the address. Does its query also check the owner?\n\n*Hint:* search your files for `$_GET['id']`.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- CSRF tricks your browser into sending a request, using your automatic login cookie.\n- Fix CSRF with a secret random token in every form, plus SameSite cookies.\n- Broken Access Control means the site forgets to check who is allowed; it is OWASP A01.\n- IDOR is changing an ID in the address to reach someone else's record.\n- Always check ownership and roles on the server, for every request.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhy can another website not simply copy your form's CSRF token?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nBecause of the browser's **Same-Origin Policy**: one site cannot read the pages of another site. The token is only in your portal's pages and session, so the other site never sees it.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA library site shows loans at `loans.php?member=55`. The query is:\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM loans WHERE member_id = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$_GET['member']]);\n```\n\nIt uses a prepared statement. Is it safe? Explain and fix it.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nIt is safe from SQL injection but **not** from IDOR. Anyone can change `55` to another number and see that member's loans. Use the logged-in member's ID from the session instead of the address:\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"SELECT * FROM loans WHERE member_id = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$_SESSION['member_id']]);\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nBuild a protected \"Delete my project\" action for a science-fair site.\n\n1. Write the form, with a CSRF token.\n2. Write the handler that checks the token.\n3. Make sure a student can only delete **their own** project.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The form:\n\n```php\n<form action=\"delete-project.php\" method=\"POST\">\n  <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"csrf_token\"\n         value=\"<?= htmlspecialchars($_SESSION['csrf_token']) ?>\">\n  <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"project_id\" value=\"<?= (int) $project['id'] ?>\">\n  <button type=\"submit\">Delete my project</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n2. and 3. The handler:\n\n```php\n<?php\nsession_start();\nif (!hash_equals($_SESSION['csrf_token'] ?? '', $_POST['csrf_token'] ?? '')) {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('Form expired. Please reload and try again.');\n}\n\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"DELETE FROM projects WHERE id = ? AND student_id = ?\");\n$stmt->execute([$_POST['project_id'] ?? 0, $_SESSION['user_id']]);\n\necho $stmt->rowCount() === 1 ? 'Project deleted.' : 'Nothing was deleted.';\n```\n\nThe `AND student_id = ?` part means another student's project can never be deleted, even if someone changes the hidden `project_id`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-web-application-vulnerabilities/ch09-security-headers.md": "# Chapter 9: HTTP Security Headers 📨\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen the school office sends a parcel of exam papers to another school, it does not just send the box. It sticks labels on the outside: **\"Fragile\"**, **\"Open only in the exam office\"**, **\"Do not forward\"**. The delivery driver never opens the box, but follows every label. A web page travels the same way. Along with the page, the server sends short labels called **headers**, and the browser obeys them. A few well-chosen labels can stop whole families of attacks before they start.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what HTTP **response headers** are and where to see them.\n- Name the main security headers and say what each one blocks.\n- Write a simple, safe **Content Security Policy**.\n- Turn on HSTS carefully, and set the **Secure**, **HttpOnly** and **SameSite** cookie flags.\n- Send security headers from PHP with `header()` and from Apache with `.htaccess`.\n\n## 📨 What Are Response Headers?\n\nWhen your browser asks for a page, it sends a **request**. The server replies with a **response**. The response has two parts:\n\n```\nBrowser                         Server\n   │  request: \"GET /index.php\"    │\n   │──────────────────────────────►│\n   │                               │\n   │  response:                    │\n   │◄──────────────────────────────│\n   │  1. headers (labels)          │\n   │  2. body (the HTML page)      │\n```\n\n**Headers** are short lines of the form `Name: value`. The visitor never sees them on the page. They tell the browser how to handle what comes next. For example:\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'\n```\n\nThe first line says the request worked (`200 OK`). `Content-Type` says \"this is an HTML page\". The third line is a **security header**: a rule the browser enforces to protect the visitor.\n\nIn Chapter 6 you met one security header already: the Content Security Policy against XSS. In Chapter 8 you met the `Set-Cookie` header with `SameSite`. This chapter puts all the important ones together.\n\n## 🔍 How to See a Site's Headers\n\n### In the browser (DevTools)\n\n1. Open the website in Chrome, Edge or Firefox.\n2. Press **F12** to open **DevTools** (the browser's built-in developer tools).\n3. Click the **Network** tab, then reload the page (**F5**).\n4. Click the first row in the list. It is the page itself.\n5. Open **Headers** and look under **Response Headers**.\n\n### In the terminal (curl)\n\n`curl` is a small program that fetches web addresses. The `-I` option asks only for the headers. In Windows PowerShell, type `curl.exe`, because plain `curl` may run a different PowerShell command.\n\n```powershell\ncurl.exe -I https://example.com\n```\n\nOutput (taken on 13 October 2026; dates and some lines change each time):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nDate: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:19:21 GMT\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8\nConnection: keep-alive\nServer: cloudflare\nlast-modified: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:18:28 GMT\nallow: GET, HEAD\nAccept-Ranges: bytes\nAge: 3972\ncf-cache-status: HIT\nCF-RAY: a49ea2ad5ed4a7f1-DEL\nalt-svc: h3=\":443\"; ma=86400\n```\n\n`example.com` is a simple demonstration page, so it sends **none** of the security headers in this chapter. By the end, you will spot that at a glance.\n\n> 🛡️ **Amānah rule:** looking at headers is harmless, because every browser receives them. Testing a site for weaknesses is different: only test sites you own or have written permission to test.\n\n## 🧱 Content-Security-Policy (CSP)\n\n**What it blocks:** scripts and other files from places you did not approve. It is a strong second layer against **XSS** (Chapter 6).\n\nA CSP is a list of rules. Each rule is called a **directive** and ends with `;`. Here is a simple, safe policy for a small school site that keeps all its files on its own server:\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\n```\n\n| Directive | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `default-src 'self'` | Scripts, styles, images and fonts may load only from this same site |\n| `object-src 'none'` | No old plug-in content (`<object>`, `<embed>`) at all |\n| `base-uri 'self'` | Nobody can insert a `<base>` tag that redirects your links elsewhere |\n| `frame-ancestors 'none'` | No other site may show this page inside a frame |\n\nWith `'self'`, scripts written **inside** the HTML (`<script>...</script>` and `onclick=\"...\"`) are blocked. Only `.js` files from your own site run. That is exactly what stops an injected script.\n\n### Nonces: allowing one script you trust\n\nSometimes a page really needs one small inline script. A **nonce** (\"number used once\") is a random value the server makes fresh for **every** page load. It puts the value in the header and on the trusted script tag:\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: script-src 'self' 'nonce-k7Qz2pXb9L'\n```\n\n```html\n<script nonce=\"k7Qz2pXb9L\">\n  document.getElementById('year').textContent = '2026';\n</script>\n```\n\nAn attacker cannot guess the next nonce, so their injected script has no matching value and is blocked. In PHP you would make it with `bin2hex(random_bytes(16))`, just like the CSRF token in Chapter 8. Never reuse the same nonce on every page.\n\n> 💡 **Test first:** send the same policy as `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`. The browser then only reports problems in the DevTools **Console** and blocks nothing. When the console is clean, switch to the real header.\n\n## 🔒 Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS)\n\n**What it blocks:** someone on the same Wi-Fi quietly switching your visit from `https://` to unprotected `http://`.\n\n```http\nStrict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\n```\n\n- **HSTS** tells the browser: \"For this site, use HTTPS only. Never plain HTTP.\"\n- `max-age` is how long the browser remembers this rule, in seconds. 31,536,000 seconds = 365 days.\n- `includeSubDomains` applies the rule to every subdomain too, such as `library.example.org`.\n- Browsers ignore this header if it arrives over plain HTTP. It only works on an HTTPS response.\n\n### Be careful with HSTS\n\nA browser that has seen HSTS will **refuse** plain HTTP for the whole `max-age`. If one subdomain has no HTTPS yet, it stops working for those visitors.\n\n1. Make sure HTTPS works everywhere first.\n2. Start small: `max-age=300` (5 minutes). Then raise it step by step to a year.\n3. Add `includeSubDomains` only when **every** subdomain has HTTPS.\n4. Do not add the extra `preload` word until you are sure. It puts your site on a list built into browsers, and leaving that list takes months.\n\n## 📄 X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\n\n**What it blocks:** the browser \"guessing\" a file's type and running it as something dangerous.\n\n```http\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\n```\n\nEvery file is sent with a `Content-Type`, such as `text/plain` or `image/png`. Some browsers used to **sniff**: look inside a file and guess its real type. Imagine a student uploads a \"homework.txt\" that actually contains JavaScript. A sniffing browser might run it as a script. `nosniff` says: \"Trust the `Content-Type` I gave you. Do not guess.\" The browser then refuses to run a script or style sheet with the wrong type.\n\nThis header has only one value, `nosniff`, and is safe to send on every response.\n\n## 🖼️ Clickjacking: frame-ancestors and X-Frame-Options\n\n**What it blocks:** **clickjacking**. Another site loads your page invisibly inside a frame and places it over its own buttons. The visitor thinks they are clicking \"Next\", but they are really clicking \"Delete my project\" on your site.\n\n```\n Other site's page (what you see)\n ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n │  [ Next ]                    │\n │   ▲ your real page sits here │\n │   │ invisible, on top:       │\n │  [ Delete my project ]       │\n └──────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nThe modern defence is the CSP directive you already saw:\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'\n```\n\nUse `'none'` (no site may frame the page) or `'self'` (only your own site may). Older browsers understand an older header that does the same job:\n\n```http\nX-Frame-Options: DENY\n```\n\n`DENY` matches `'none'`, and `SAMEORIGIN` matches `'self'`. Sending both is common and harmless. When both are present, modern browsers follow `frame-ancestors`.\n\n## 🔗 Referrer-Policy\n\n**What it blocks:** leaking the full address of your page to other sites.\n\nWhen a visitor clicks a link to another site, the browser may tell that site which page they came from. This is called the **referrer**. A full address can contain private details, such as `report?id=1042` or a password-reset code.\n\n```http\nReferrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin\n```\n\nThis sends the full address within your own site, but only the site name (`https://school-portal.example.org`) to other sites, and nothing at all from HTTPS to HTTP. Modern browsers already use this as their default, but setting it yourself makes it certain. For very private pages, use `no-referrer`.\n\n## 🎛️ Permissions-Policy\n\n**What it blocks:** using powerful browser features your site does not need.\n\n```http\nPermissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()\n```\n\nThe empty brackets `()` mean \"allowed for nobody\". So even if a harmful script gets into the page, it cannot ask to use the camera, the microphone or the visitor's location. A homework site needs none of these. Turn off what you do not use: that is the principle of **least privilege** (give only the access that is needed).\n\n## 🍪 Cookie Flags: Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite\n\nCookies are set with the `Set-Cookie` header, and three **flags** (extra settings) protect them:\n\n| Flag | What it blocks | Chapter |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `Secure` | Sending the cookie over plain HTTP, where it can be read on the network | this one |\n| `HttpOnly` | JavaScript reading the cookie, so XSS cannot steal the login | 6 |\n| `SameSite=Lax` | Sending the cookie with forms from other sites | 8 (CSRF) |\n\nIn PHP, set them for the session cookie **before** `session_start()`:\n\n```php\n<?php\nsession_set_cookie_params([\n    'secure'   => true,   // only over HTTPS\n    'httponly' => true,   // JavaScript cannot read it\n    'samesite' => 'Lax',  // not sent with forms from other sites\n]);\nsession_start();\necho \"Session started\\n\";\n```\n\nThe `Set-Cookie` header this sends (the session ID is random each time):\n\n```http\nSet-Cookie: PHPSESSID=ssi4emhp1dnd1gat7828sj055b; path=/; secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\n## 🗑️ Old Headers You Can Skip\n\nOld tutorials often list headers that modern browsers no longer use:\n\n- **`X-XSS-Protection`** switched on an \"XSS filter\" built into old browsers. Edge removed that filter in 2018 and Chrome in 2019; Firefox never had it. The filter could even be misused to cause harm. **Do not rely on it.** Leave the header out, or send `X-XSS-Protection: 0` to turn the old filter off. Your real XSS defences are escaping (Chapter 6) and CSP.\n- **`Public-Key-Pins`** and **`Expect-CT`** are also retired. Browsers ignore them.\n\n## 🐘 Setting Headers in PHP\n\nPHP's `header()` function adds one response header. It must run **before** any HTML or `echo`, because headers travel in front of the page.\n\n```php\n<?php\n// Security headers: send them before any HTML\nheader(\"Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\");\nheader('X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff');\nheader('X-Frame-Options: DENY');\nheader('Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin');\nheader('Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()');\n?>\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head><meta charset=\"UTF-8\"><title>Library</title></head>\n<body><h1>Green Valley Library</h1></body>\n</html>\n```\n\nRun this page with PHP's built-in test server (`php -S 127.0.0.1:8137`) and ask for its headers with `curl.exe -I http://127.0.0.1:8137/index.php`. Output:\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nHost: 127.0.0.1:8137\nDate: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:19:30 GMT\nConnection: close\nX-Powered-By: PHP/8.3.31\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nX-Frame-Options: DENY\nReferrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin\nPermissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()\nContent-type: text/html; charset=UTF-8\n```\n\nEvery header you set is there. HSTS is left out of this test on purpose, because the test server uses plain HTTP. Add it only on a real HTTPS site.\n\nNotice `X-Powered-By: PHP/8.3.31`. It tells everyone your exact PHP version, which helps attackers look up known weaknesses. On a real server, set `expose_php = Off` in `php.ini` to hide it.\n\n## 🪶 Setting Headers in Apache (.htaccess)\n\nIf your site runs on Apache (as in XAMPP), you can set the headers once for a whole folder. Apache's **mod_headers** module adds the `Header` command. Put this in a file named `.htaccess` in your site's folder:\n\n```apache\n<IfModule mod_headers.c>\n  Header set Content-Security-Policy \"default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\"\n  Header set X-Content-Type-Options \"nosniff\"\n  Header set X-Frame-Options \"DENY\"\n  Header set Referrer-Policy \"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\"\n  Header set Permissions-Policy \"camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()\"\n</IfModule>\n```\n\n- `<IfModule mod_headers.c>` means \"only if mod_headers is switched on\". XAMPP loads it by default.\n- `Header set` adds the header, replacing any old value with the same name.\n- Write `Header always set` if you also want the header on error pages, such as \"404 Not Found\".\n- Add the HSTS line only on a site that already runs on HTTPS.\n\nChoose **one** place for each header, PHP or Apache. Setting the same header in both can send it twice.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Open your own project in DevTools → Network and list which security headers it sends.\n- Add CSP, `nosniff`, `frame-ancestors` (plus `X-Frame-Options`), Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy.\n- Test a new CSP with `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` first.\n- Mark session cookies `Secure`, `HttpOnly` and `SameSite=Lax`.\n- Turn on HSTS only after HTTPS works everywhere, starting with a small `max-age`.\n- Skip `X-XSS-Protection`; it is obsolete.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake 1: calling `header()` after output.**\n\n```php\n<?php\necho \"<h1>Welcome</h1>\\n\";\nheader('X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff');\n```\n\n**What happens:** the page starts printing, so the headers have already gone. PHP gives a warning and the header is **not** sent. Running `php late.php` in the terminal prints:\n\n```\n<h1>Welcome</h1>\nPHP Warning:  Cannot modify header information - headers already sent by (output started at ...\\late.php:2) in ...\\late.php on line 3\n\nWarning: Cannot modify header information - headers already sent by (output started at ...\\late.php:2) in ...\\late.php on line 3\n```\n\n(The `...` stands for the folder on your computer.) Even a blank line or a space before `<?php` counts as output.\n\n**Fixed version:** send every header first, then the page.\n\n```php\n<?php\nheader('X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff');\necho \"<h1>Welcome</h1>\\n\";\n```\n\n**Mistake 2: turning on HSTS for a year on day one.**\n\nA student adds `max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` while `old.example.org` still has no HTTPS. For a whole year, visitors' browsers refuse to open that subdomain. **Fixed version:** start with `max-age=300`, check every subdomain, then raise it slowly.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCompare two websites you use for school (for example, your school's site and an online library).\n\n1. Open each in DevTools → **Network**, click the first row, and read the **Response Headers**.\n2. Make a small table: for each site, write ✓ or ✗ for CSP, HSTS, `nosniff`, `frame-ancestors` or `X-Frame-Options`, Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy.\n3. Does either site still send `X-XSS-Protection`?\n\n*Hint:* header names are not case-sensitive, so `strict-transport-security` and `Strict-Transport-Security` are the same header.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Response headers are `Name: value` labels sent before the page; the browser obeys them.\n- CSP limits where scripts and files may come from; nonces allow one trusted inline script.\n- HSTS forces HTTPS for `max-age` seconds; start small and be careful with `includeSubDomains`.\n- `nosniff` stops type guessing; `frame-ancestors` and `X-Frame-Options` stop clickjacking.\n- Referrer-Policy keeps addresses private; Permissions-Policy turns off unneeded features; cookies need `Secure`, `HttpOnly` and `SameSite`.\n- Set headers with PHP `header()` before any output, or with `Header set` in `.htaccess`. `X-XSS-Protection` is obsolete.\n\n## 📖 Key Terms\n\n| Term | What It Means |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Response header** | A `Name: value` line the server sends before the page body. |\n| **CSP** | Content Security Policy: rules about where scripts and other files may load from. |\n| **Nonce** | A random value made fresh for each page load, used to allow one trusted script. |\n| **HSTS** | Strict-Transport-Security: makes the browser use HTTPS only for a set time. |\n| **MIME sniffing** | A browser guessing a file's type by looking inside it. |\n| **Clickjacking** | Hiding a real page in an invisible frame to trick people into clicking it. |\n| **Referrer** | The address of the page a visitor came from. |\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nMatch each header to the attack or problem it helps block:\n\n1. `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`\n2. `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'`\n3. `Strict-Transport-Security`\n4. `Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'`\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. The browser guessing a file's type and running it as a script (MIME sniffing).\n2. Clickjacking: other sites cannot show your page in a frame.\n3. Being switched from HTTPS to plain HTTP on the network.\n4. Injected inline scripts (XSS), because only script files from your own site may run.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA classmate's PHP page sends these headers:\n\n```http\nX-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block\nSet-Cookie: session=7f3a9c; path=/\n```\n\nExplain what is wrong and write better headers.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- `X-XSS-Protection` is obsolete. Modern browsers ignore it, so it gives no real protection. Remove it (or send `0`) and use a real CSP.\n- The cookie has no flags. It can travel over plain HTTP, be read by JavaScript and be sent with forms from other sites.\n\nBetter:\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nSet-Cookie: session=7f3a9c; path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYour school's science-fair site runs on HTTPS with Apache. It has two subdomains: `fair.example.org` (HTTPS) and `archive.example.org` (still plain HTTP).\n\n1. Write an `.htaccess` block that sends CSP, `nosniff`, both clickjacking headers, Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy (no camera, microphone or location).\n2. Add an HSTS line that is safe for **now**.\n3. Explain what must happen before you can use `includeSubDomains` and a one-year `max-age`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. and 2.\n\n```apache\n<IfModule mod_headers.c>\n  Header set Content-Security-Policy \"default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\"\n  Header set X-Content-Type-Options \"nosniff\"\n  Header set X-Frame-Options \"DENY\"\n  Header set Referrer-Policy \"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\"\n  Header set Permissions-Policy \"camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()\"\n  Header set Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=300\"\n</IfModule>\n```\n\n3. `archive.example.org` must get HTTPS first. With `includeSubDomains`, browsers would refuse to open it over plain HTTP, so it would break for visitors. Once every subdomain works on HTTPS, raise `max-age` step by step (for example a day, a month, then `31536000`) and add `includeSubDomains`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-cryptography-and-secure-channels/ch10-hashing-vs-encryption-vs-encoding.md": "# Chapter 10: Hashing vs. Encryption vs. Encoding 🔐\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThink of three things you might do with a school letter. You could **translate** it into Urdu so your grandmother can read it. Anyone who knows both languages can translate it back. You could **lock it in a box** and give the key only to your teacher. Only someone with the key can open it. Or you could **press your thumb in ink** at the bottom. The thumbprint does not contain the letter, but it proves the letter is yours and that nobody swapped the page. Translating, locking and fingerprinting: in computing, these are **encoding**, **encryption** and **hashing**.\n\nPeople mix up these three all the time. Using the wrong one can leave data wide open.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Tell apart **encoding**, **encryption** and **hashing** by purpose, key and reversibility.\n- Show why Base64 is not a way to hide secrets.\n- Explain **symmetric** and **asymmetric** encryption in simple words.\n- Describe the properties of a good hash, including the **avalanche effect**.\n\n## 🧭 The Three at a Glance\n\n| | Purpose | Needs a key? | Can it be reversed? | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Encoding** | Change the format so data travels safely | No | Yes, by anyone | Base64, URL encoding |\n| **Encryption** | Keep data secret | Yes | Yes, but only with the key | AES, RSA, TLS |\n| **Hashing** | Make a fingerprint to check data | No | No (one-way) | SHA-256; bcrypt and Argon2id for passwords |\n\n## 🔤 Encoding: Changing the Format (Not Security)\n\n**Encoding** changes data into another format so it can travel without being damaged. For example, **Base64** turns any bytes, such as a picture, into plain letters and digits that can sit inside an email or a text file.\n\n```php\n<?php\necho base64_encode('Hello'), \"\\n\";\necho base64_decode('SGVsbG8='), \"\\n\";\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nSGVsbG8=\nHello\n```\n\n> ⚠️ **Base64 is not encryption.** There is no key. Anyone can decode it in a second with free tools. If you see a \"hidden\" password stored in Base64, it is not hidden at all.\n\nAnother common encoding is **URL encoding**: a space becomes `%20` so it can travel safely in a web address.\n\n## 🔒 Encryption: A Lock and a Key\n\n**Encryption** turns readable data (the **plaintext**) into scrambled data (the **ciphertext**) using a **key**. Only someone with the right key can turn it back.\n\n### Symmetric encryption: one shared key\nThe same key locks and unlocks. **AES** is the most widely used method. It is fast and very strong. Here is AES in PHP:\n\n```php\n<?php\n$key = random_bytes(32);          // a secret 256-bit key\n$iv  = random_bytes(12);          // a fresh random starting value\n$secret = 'Meet at the library at 4';\n\n$cipher = openssl_encrypt($secret, 'aes-256-gcm', $key, 0, $iv, $tag);\n\necho openssl_decrypt($cipher, 'aes-256-gcm', $key, 0, $iv, $tag), \"\\n\";\nvar_dump(openssl_decrypt($cipher, 'aes-256-gcm', random_bytes(32), 0, $iv, $tag));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nMeet at the library at 4\nbool(false)\n```\n\nWith the right key, the message comes back. With a wrong key, decryption fails (`false`). The scrambled `$cipher` itself looks like random characters and changes on every run, because the starting value `$iv` is new each time.\n\nThe hard part of symmetric encryption is sharing the key safely. If you send the key along with the message, anyone who catches both can read it.\n\n### Asymmetric encryption: a padlock and its key\n**Asymmetric** encryption uses a **pair** of keys:\n\n- A **public key**, which you can give to anyone. It works like an open padlock: anyone can snap it shut on a box.\n- A **private key**, which you never share. Only it can open that padlock.\n\nRSA and elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC) work this way. It is slower than AES, so real systems combine them. When you visit an HTTPS website, **TLS** uses asymmetric methods to agree a fresh shared key safely, then uses fast symmetric encryption for everything else. Chapter 11 shows why this matters on public Wi-Fi.\n\n## 👆 Hashing: A One-Way Fingerprint\n\nA **hash function** takes input of any length and produces a short, fixed-size fingerprint. SHA-256 always produces 256 bits, shown as **64 hexadecimal characters** (the digits 0–9 and letters a–f).\n\n```php\n<?php\necho hash('sha256', 'Bismillah'), \"\\n\";\necho hash('sha256', 'Bismillah!'), \"\\n\";\necho strlen(hash('sha256', 'A whole library of books')), \"\\n\";\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n1c1b72aee1bf62759f34d254e0fb8ce2dafb480d42bc5ab1f5af0bdb6e53d108\n0f4fda5d526f94ba2c244a5c7a5bc4d576300a68d9c821bca26dfb716ed0bf74\n64\n```\n\nAdding one `!` changed 56 of the 64 characters. A long sentence still gives exactly 64 characters.\n\n### Four properties of a good hash\n1. **Same in, same out:** the same input always gives the same hash.\n2. **One-way:** you cannot work backwards from the hash to the input. It is like baking: you cannot turn a cake back into eggs and flour.\n3. **Avalanche effect:** a tiny change in the input changes the hash completely, as you just saw.\n4. **Collision resistant:** it is practically impossible to find two different inputs with the same hash. (Collisions must exist in theory, because there are endless inputs and a limited number of hashes. Nobody has ever found one for SHA-256.)\n\n### What hashes are used for\n- **Checking downloads:** a website publishes the SHA-256 of a file. After downloading, you hash your copy. If the two match, the file was not damaged or swapped.\n- **Storing passwords:** but only with a **slow, salted** password hash such as bcrypt or Argon2id, as in Chapter 2. Plain SHA-256 is too fast for passwords.\n\n## 🧩 Choosing the Right Tool\n\n| Job | Use |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Put an image inside a text message format | Encoding (Base64) |\n| Send a private message only the receiver can read | Encryption |\n| Store user passwords | Password hashing (`password_hash()`) |\n| Check a download was not changed | Hashing (SHA-256) |\n| Put a space in a web address | Encoding (URL encoding) |\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Never use Base64 to \"hide\" anything secret.\n- Store passwords only with `password_hash()`, never with Base64 or plain SHA-256.\n- Use HTTPS for every site you build.\n- Keep encryption keys out of your code files and out of Git.\n- When a download page shows a SHA-256 value, check it.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** a student stores passwords like this, thinking it is \"encrypted\":\n\n```php\n// ❌ Encoding is not protection\n$stored = base64_encode($password);\n```\n\n**What happens:** anyone who sees the database can run `base64_decode()` and read every password in seconds.\n\n**Fixed version:**\n\n```php\n// ✅ Slow, salted, one-way password hash\n$stored = password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);\n```\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen the **Crypto** tab in the Cybersecurity Lab Studio.\n\n1. Type a short word and look at its SHA-256 hash.\n2. Change one letter, or add one full stop.\n3. Count roughly how many of the 64 characters changed.\n\n*Hint:* compare the first ten characters of each hash.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- **Encoding** changes the format; anyone can reverse it; it is not security.\n- **Encryption** keeps data secret; it can be reversed only with the key.\n- Symmetric encryption uses one shared key (AES); asymmetric uses a public and private pair (RSA, ECC).\n- **Hashing** makes a one-way, fixed-size fingerprint; a tiny change makes a totally different hash.\n- Passwords need slow, salted password hashes, not encoding and not plain SHA-256.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich of the three (encoding, encryption, hashing) can be reversed, and what is needed to reverse it?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- **Encoding:** reversible by anyone; no key needed.\n- **Encryption:** reversible only with the correct key.\n- **Hashing:** not reversible; it is one-way.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA website stores this as a user's password: `b2xpdmUgdHJlZXM=`. It ends in `=` and uses only letters, digits, `+`, `/` and `=`. What is it most likely, and why is it a problem?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nIt is most likely **Base64 encoding**. It decodes to `olive trees`. Anyone who sees it can read the real password, because encoding needs no key. The site should use `password_hash()` instead.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA school club website does three jobs. For each, choose encoding, encryption or hashing, and explain your choice in one sentence.\n\n1. Saving members' passwords.\n2. Keeping members' medical notes private in the database, readable later by the club nurse.\n3. Checking that the club's setup file, downloaded from the internet, has not been changed.\n\nThen write the PHP line for job 1.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **Password hashing**: the site never needs the real password back, only to check it.\n2. **Encryption**: the nurse must be able to read the notes again, so it must be reversible, but only with a key.\n3. **Hashing (SHA-256)**: compare the file's hash with the one published by the maker.\n\n```php\n$stored = password_hash($_POST['password'], PASSWORD_DEFAULT);\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-cryptography-and-secure-channels/ch11-vpn-public-wifi-and-traffic-sniffing.md": "# Chapter 11: Public Wi-Fi, VPNs & Traffic Sniffing 📡\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nImagine sending a **postcard**. Every person who handles it, from the postbox to the sorting office to the delivery van, can read what you wrote. Now imagine putting the card in a **sealed envelope**. People can still see the address on the front, but not the message inside. Finally, imagine putting that envelope inside a **locked courier bag** that only opens at the courier's office in another city. People on the way cannot even see the final address. Data on the internet can travel in all three ways: as a postcard (HTTP), an envelope (HTTPS) or a courier bag (a VPN).\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why open public Wi-Fi can let others see or change your traffic.\n- Describe **sniffing**, **man-in-the-middle** and **evil twin** attacks in simple words.\n- Explain what HTTPS protects, and what it does not hide.\n- Describe what a **VPN** does, and how to choose and use one wisely.\n\n## 📡 Wi-Fi Is a Shared Room\n\nWi-Fi sends data by radio. Every device in range \"hears\" the radio signals, a bit like people in one room hearing every conversation. Normally a device ignores messages not meant for it. But with special software, a device can listen to everything nearby. This is called **sniffing** (or packet sniffing). A **packet** is a small piece of data travelling across a network.\n\nOn an **open** network (one with no Wi-Fi password), the radio signals themselves are not encrypted. On those networks, anything sent as plain HTTP could be read by someone nearby.\n\n```\n[ Your phone ] ── plain HTTP ──► [ Router ]\n                   │\n                   │ (radio heard by\n                   ▼  everyone nearby)\n          [ Someone listening ]\n          can read and copy it\n```\n\nNetwork staff use sniffing tools, such as Wireshark, to fix problems on networks **they are responsible for**. Capturing other people's traffic without permission is illegal.\n\n## 🕵️ Two Tricks to Know\n\n### Man-in-the-middle (MITM)\nA **man-in-the-middle** attack means someone secretly sits between you and the website. They pass messages back and forth, so everything seems normal. But they can read, and sometimes change, what passes through.\n\n### The evil twin\nAn **evil twin** is a fake Wi-Fi hotspot with the same name as a real one, such as `City_Library_Free_WiFi`. Phones often join known names automatically. Once you are connected, all your traffic flows through the faker's device: a perfect man-in-the-middle.\n\n**Defence:** ask staff for the exact network name. Turn off \"auto-join\" for public networks. Forget networks you no longer use.\n\n## 🔒 How HTTPS Protects You\n\n**HTTPS** is HTTP inside an encrypted connection called **TLS** (Transport Layer Security). You learned in Chapter 10 that TLS agrees a secret key and then encrypts everything. TLS also checks the website's **certificate**: a digital ID card that proves the site really owns its name. That check makes man-in-the-middle tricks fail. Your browser shows a big warning instead.\n\nHere is what a listener on the same Wi-Fi can see:\n\n| What is visible? | Plain HTTP | HTTPS | HTTPS through a VPN |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Page content, forms, passwords | ✅ Yes | ❌ No | ❌ No |\n| Full address, e.g. `/results?id=42` | ✅ Yes | ❌ No | ❌ No |\n| Site name, e.g. `library.example.org` | ✅ Yes | Usually yes | ❌ No (only the VPN server) |\n| That you are online, and how much data | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n\nSo with HTTPS, the network can usually see *which site* you visit, but not *what you do there*.\n\n> ⚠️ **Never click past a certificate warning** on public Wi-Fi. It may be the exact moment a man-in-the-middle is trying to step in.\n\n## 🚇 VPNs: An Encrypted Tunnel\n\nA **VPN** (Virtual Private Network) creates an encrypted tunnel from your device to a VPN server somewhere else. All your traffic goes through the tunnel first.\n\n```\n[ You ] ══ encrypted ══► [ VPN server ]\n             tunnel            │\n                               ▼\n                         [ Internet ]\n```\n\n### What a VPN does\n- **Protects you on public Wi-Fi:** the local network sees only scrambled data going to the VPN server.\n- **Hides your home internet address (IP address)** from the websites you visit. They see the VPN server's address instead.\n- Organisations use VPNs so staff can reach internal systems safely from home.\n\n### What a VPN does not do\n- It does **not** make you anonymous. The VPN company can see where your traffic goes, so you are moving your trust from the Wi-Fi owner to the VPN company.\n- It does **not** stop phishing, malware or weak passwords.\n- It does **not** replace HTTPS. Use both.\n\n> ⚠️ **Choosing a VPN (*Amānah*)**: many \"free VPN\" apps pay for themselves by collecting and selling what you do online. Choose a well-known provider whose privacy claims have been checked by independent auditors, or the VPN your school provides. Follow your school's and family's rules about VPN use.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Set your home router to **WPA3**, or **WPA2 (AES)** if WPA3 is not available. Never use WEP or an open network.\n- Change the router's default admin password.\n- On public Wi-Fi, use only HTTPS sites and avoid logging in to important accounts if you can.\n- Use your phone's mobile data for anything sensitive when unsure.\n- Turn off auto-join for public networks.\n- Never ignore certificate warnings.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"The café Wi-Fi has a password printed on the wall, so it is private.\"\n\n**What happens:** everyone in the café has the same password. Anyone on that network can still try tricks such as setting up an evil twin with the same name and password.\n\n**Fixed thinking:** treat every shared network as public. Rely on HTTPS (and a trusted VPN if you have one), not on the Wi-Fi password.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nWith a parent's permission, check your home Wi-Fi security.\n\n1. On Windows, open **Settings → Network & Internet → Wi-Fi → (your network) properties**.\n2. Find the **Security type**.\n3. Write it down: is it WPA3, WPA2 or something older?\n\n*Hint:* on a phone, the security type is often shown in the network details when you tap the network name.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Wi-Fi is like a shared room: nearby devices can hear the radio signals.\n- Sniffing, man-in-the-middle and evil twin attacks target unprotected traffic.\n- HTTPS encrypts content and checks the site's certificate, but the site name is usually still visible.\n- A VPN tunnels all traffic to a VPN server; it moves trust to the VPN company and does not make you anonymous.\n- Use WPA3 or WPA2 at home and never click past certificate warnings.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is an \"evil twin\" Wi-Fi network?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA fake Wi-Fi hotspot that copies the name of a real network, so that devices join it and send all their traffic through the faker's device.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nHamza is on the open library Wi-Fi. He visits `https://school.example.org/marks`. Someone nearby is sniffing. What can they probably see, and what can they not see?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**Can probably see:** that Hamza is connected, how much data he uses, and the site name `school.example.org`.\n\n**Cannot see:** the page path `/marks`, his password, his marks or anything he types, because HTTPS encrypts them.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWrite a \"Safe Public Wi-Fi\" card for students going on a school trip.\n\n1. Give at least five short rules.\n2. Explain in one sentence what HTTPS does and what it does not hide.\n3. Explain in one sentence what a VPN adds, and one thing it does not do.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n**Safe Public Wi-Fi**\n1. Ask staff for the exact network name.\n2. Turn off auto-join for public networks.\n3. Only use sites that show HTTPS.\n4. Never click past a certificate warning.\n5. Save important logins for mobile data or home.\n6. Log out when you finish, and forget the network afterwards.\n\n**HTTPS** hides what you send and read, but people on the network can usually still see which site you visit.\n\n**A VPN** hides even the site name from the local network, but it does not stop phishing or make you anonymous to the VPN company.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-digital-hygiene-and-privacy/ch12-device-hygiene-malware-and-updates.md": "# Chapter 12: Device Hygiene, Malware & The Update Imperative 🧹\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWashing your hands, cleaning a cut and eating wholesome food keep you healthy. None of them is exciting, but together they prevent most illness. Your devices need the same kind of daily care. Ibrahim's family learned this the hard way. Their laptop showed \"Update available\" for months, and they always clicked \"Later\". Then one morning every photo and homework file was locked, with a message demanding payment. The update they kept postponing would have closed the hole that let the harmful program in.\n\n**Digital hygiene** means the small, regular habits that keep devices and data healthy.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Name the main kinds of **malware** and how each one spreads.\n- Explain why updates matter, including **zero-day** and known vulnerabilities.\n- Follow the **3-2-1 backup rule** and test a backup.\n- Build a simple device-hygiene routine.\n\n## 🦠 Malware: Software Built to Harm\n\n**Malware** (short for \"malicious software\") is any program made to damage, spy on or take control of a device without the owner's permission.\n\n| Type | What it does | How it usually arrives |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Virus** | Attaches to a real program or file and spreads when that is opened | Shared files, infected downloads |\n| **Worm** | Copies itself across a network on its own, with no click needed | Unpatched computers on the same network |\n| **Trojan** | Pretends to be something useful, but secretly lets an attacker in | \"Free\" copies of paid software, fake tools |\n| **Ransomware** | Encrypts your files and demands payment for the key | Phishing attachments, unpatched systems |\n| **Spyware / keylogger** | Secretly records what you type or do and sends it away | Bundled with fake apps or browser add-ons |\n\nThe name **Trojan** comes from the wooden horse in Chapter 3: something welcomed inside as a gift that turns out to be a trap.\n\n### Warning signs that something is wrong\n- The device suddenly becomes very slow or very hot.\n- New toolbars, add-ons or apps appear that you did not install.\n- Your antivirus has been switched off and you did not do it.\n- Friends receive strange messages \"from you\".\n\n**What to do:** disconnect from the internet, tell a parent or teacher, and run a full antivirus scan. Then change important passwords **from a different, healthy device**.\n\n### Risky vs safe habits\n\n| ❌ Risky | ✅ Safe |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Downloading a \"free full version\" of a paid app from a random site | Using the official app store or the maker's website |\n| Clicking \"Allow\" on every pop-up | Reading what an app asks for, and saying no if unsure |\n| Turning antivirus off \"because it is slow\" | Keeping built-in protection on, such as Microsoft Defender |\n| Plugging in a USB stick found on the floor | Handing it to the office |\n\nUsing pirated software is also dishonest: it takes the work of others without permission. Free, open-source and school-provided software is a halal and safe choice.\n\n## 🔄 Why Updates Matter\n\nAll software is written by people, so all software has bugs. Some bugs are **vulnerabilities**: weaknesses an attacker could use to get in.\n\n- A **zero-day** is a vulnerability that the maker does not know about yet, or has had \"zero days\" to fix.\n- Once the maker finds it, they write a fix, called a **patch**, and send it out as an **update**.\n\n```\n1. Weakness found\n        │\n        ▼\n2. Maker writes a patch\n        │\n        ▼\n3. Update sent to users\n        │\n   ┌────┴─────┐\n   ▼          ▼\nUpdated:   Postponed:\nsafe       still open\n```\n\nHere is the surprising part. When an update is released, attackers can study the patch to learn exactly what was broken. They then target everyone who has **not** updated yet. In fact, many real break-ins use weaknesses that already had a fix available. Updating quickly is one of the most effective things you can do.\n\n> 💡 **Core rule:** turn on **automatic updates** for your operating system, browser, apps and router.\n\nAlso watch for **end of support**. When a maker stops sending security updates for an old version, that device stays open to every new weakness. Plan to replace or upgrade it.\n\n## 💾 The 3-2-1 Backup Rule\n\nHardware breaks, laptops get lost, and ransomware locks files. A **backup** is an extra copy of your data kept somewhere else. The **3-2-1 rule** is a simple, widely used plan:\n\n- **3 copies** of important data: the original plus 2 backups.\n- **2 different kinds of storage**, such as the laptop's drive and an external USB drive.\n- **1 copy kept off-site**, such as in trusted cloud storage or at a relative's home.\n\n### Worked example: Fatima's science project\n\n| Copy | Where | Kind of storage | Off-site? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 (original) | Laptop | Internal drive | No |\n| 2 | USB drive, unplugged after each backup | External drive | No |\n| 3 | School's cloud storage | Online storage | Yes |\n\n3 copies ✅, 2 kinds of storage ✅, 1 off-site ✅. If ransomware hits the laptop, the **unplugged** USB drive cannot be reached by it.\n\n> ⚠️ A backup you have never restored is only a hope. Every few months, open a file from your backup to make sure it really works.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Turn on automatic updates on every device, including phones and the router.\n- Restart when updates ask you to; many only finish after a restart.\n- Keep built-in antivirus and the firewall switched on.\n- Install apps only from official stores or makers' websites.\n- Follow 3-2-1 for schoolwork and family photos, and test a restore.\n- Use a standard (non-admin) account for daily use where possible.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"My files are in a folder that syncs to the cloud, so that is my backup.\"\n\n**What happens:** syncing copies **every** change, including bad ones. If ransomware encrypts your files, or you delete a folder by accident, the sync copies that damage to the cloud too.\n\n**Fixed version:** use a backup that keeps **older versions** (many cloud services offer \"version history\"), plus a separate copy that is unplugged most of the time.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nWith a parent's permission, do a five-minute health check:\n\n1. **Windows:** open **Settings → Windows Update** and check for updates. On a Mac: **System Settings → General → Software Update**.\n2. **Windows:** open **Windows Security → Virus & threat protection** and make sure real-time protection is on.\n3. Write down where your schoolwork is backed up. Does it meet 3-2-1?\n\n*Hint:* if you can only count one copy, you have no backup yet.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Malware includes viruses, worms, Trojans, ransomware and spyware.\n- Many break-ins use weaknesses that already had a patch, so update quickly and automatically.\n- A zero-day is a weakness the maker has not fixed yet.\n- The 3-2-1 rule: 3 copies, 2 kinds of storage, 1 off-site. Keep one copy unplugged.\n- Test your backups by actually restoring a file.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat does the 3-2-1 backup rule mean?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nKeep **3** copies of important data, on **2** different kinds of storage, with **1** copy kept off-site.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nYusuf keeps his homework on his laptop and on a USB stick that is **always plugged in**. Does this follow 3-2-1? What risk does it leave?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nNo. He has only **2** copies, and **none** is off-site. Because the USB stick is always plugged in, ransomware on the laptop could encrypt it too. He should add an off-site copy (such as school cloud storage with version history) and unplug the USB stick after each backup.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWrite a monthly \"device health\" checklist for your family.\n\n1. Include at least six checks covering updates, protection and backups.\n2. For each, say how often to do it.\n3. Add what to do if a warning sign of malware appears.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n| Check | How often |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Automatic updates are on for every device | Monthly |\n| Restart devices that are waiting to finish an update | Weekly |\n| Antivirus and firewall are on | Monthly |\n| Remove apps and browser add-ons nobody uses | Monthly |\n| Backup ran; USB copy is unplugged afterwards | Weekly |\n| Restore one test file from the backup | Every 3 months |\n| Router updates checked and default password changed | Every 3 months |\n\n**If a warning sign appears:** disconnect from the internet, tell a parent, run a full scan, and change important passwords from a different device.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-digital-hygiene-and-privacy/ch13-privacy-metadata-and-digital-footprints.md": "# Chapter 13: Privacy, Metadata & Digital Footprints 👣\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen you walk across a sandy beach, you leave footprints. Someone who comes later can tell where you walked, roughly how big your feet are, and which way you went. You did not mean to share any of that. It was simply left behind. Every photo you share, every app you use and every site you visit leaves similar **digital footprints**. Some you can see. Many are hidden inside files and browsers. This chapter shows you where they are, and how to leave fewer of them.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what **metadata** is and find the hidden **EXIF** data in a photo.\n- Describe how **browser fingerprinting** can recognise a device without cookies.\n- Review app permissions and privacy settings.\n- Apply **data minimisation**: share only what is needed.\n\n## 📷 Metadata: Data About Data\n\n**Metadata** means \"data about data\". A letter's contents are the data. The postmark, date and address on the envelope are metadata. Metadata can reveal a lot, even when the contents are private.\n\n### EXIF: the hidden label inside photos\nWhen a phone takes a photo, it often stores **EXIF** (Exchangeable Image File Format) data inside the image file. Here is a **sample** of what it can hold:\n\n```\nCamera model:  Phone Model X\nDate taken:    2026-09-25 14:32:10\nGPS latitude:  51° 30' 26.5\" N\nGPS longitude:  0° 07' 39.6\" W\nSoftware:      Photos 4.2\n```\n\nThose GPS numbers are in degrees, minutes and seconds. A short JavaScript function turns them into the decimal form that map apps use:\n\n```javascript\nfunction toDecimal(deg, min, sec) {\n  return deg + min / 60 + sec / 3600;\n}\nconsole.log(toDecimal(51, 30, 26.5).toFixed(5));\nconsole.log(toDecimal(0, 7, 39.6).toFixed(5));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n51.50736\n0.12767\n```\n\nTyped into a map (with West written as minus: 51.50736, -0.12767), this points to a spot within a few metres. If that photo was taken at home and then shared publicly, it could reveal where someone lives.\n\n### How to protect yourself\n- Many phone cameras have a setting to turn off **location tags**.\n- When sharing, many phones offer an option to **remove location** first.\n- Many large social sites strip EXIF on upload, but email, chat apps that send \"original quality\" files, and personal websites often do **not**. Do not rely on it.\n\n## 🕵️ Browser Fingerprinting\n\nA **cookie** is a small file a website stores in your browser to remember you. You can delete cookies. But trackers have another method.\n\n**Browser fingerprinting** collects many small facts about your browser and device, then combines them into an ID. Each fact alone is common. Together they can be rare.\n\n- Screen size and colour depth\n- Fonts installed on the computer\n- Time zone and language\n- Small differences in how the device draws graphics\n- Browser version and operating system\n\n```\n[ screen 1920×1080 ]\n[ 42 fonts ]          ──► [ an ID that\n[ time zone +05:00 ]        often fits\n[ graphics quirks ]         only your\n                            device ]\n```\n\nIn a 2010 study by the Electronic Frontier Foundation, about 84% of the browsers tested had a unique fingerprint. A private (\"incognito\") window does **not** stop fingerprinting. It only stops your own device keeping your history and cookies after you close the window.\n\n**What helps:** browsers with built-in tracking protection, keeping the browser updated, and installing few add-ons (each one can make you more unusual).\n\n## 📱 App Permissions\n\nA **permission** is something an app asks to use, such as your location, camera, microphone or contacts.\n\n| App | Permission requested | Sensible? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Map app | Location while using | ✅ Yes, it needs it |\n| Calculator | Location | ❌ No reason |\n| Prayer-time app | Approximate location | ✅ Yes (approximate is enough) |\n| Torch app | Contacts | ❌ No reason |\n\nChoose **\"only while using the app\"** and **\"approximate location\"** wherever possible. If an app asks for something it does not need, deny it or choose a different app.\n\n## 🧺 Data Minimisation: Share Less\n\n**Data minimisation** means collecting and sharing only what is truly needed. It works in two directions.\n\n- **As a user:** do not fill in optional fields. Avoid posting your full birth date, school name, home area or daily routine publicly. Delete accounts you no longer use.\n- **As a developer (*Amānah*):** if your form does not need a phone number, do not ask for one. Data you never collect can never leak.\n\n### Risky vs safer post\n\n| ❌ Risky | ✅ Safer |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| \"Walking home from Greenfield School like every day at 3:15! 🏠\" + a photo outside the house | \"Lovely autumn walk today 🍂\" with no location, posted later |\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\n- Turn off location tags in your camera app, or remove location before sharing.\n- Review app permissions every few months and remove what is not needed.\n- Use a browser with tracking protection and keep it updated.\n- Do not share your full name, school, address or routine publicly.\n- Delete old accounts you no longer use.\n- Search for your own name with a parent, to see what others can find.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"I use incognito mode, so nobody can track me.\"\n\n**What happens:** incognito only stops *your own browser* keeping history and cookies after you close the window. Websites, your school or home network, and fingerprinting can still see and recognise your activity.\n\n**Fixed thinking:** private windows are useful on a shared computer. Real privacy comes from sharing less, managing permissions and using tracking protection.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCheck a photo on a computer (with permission):\n\n1. **Windows:** right-click an image file, choose **Properties**, then the **Details** tab.\n2. Look for the camera model, date taken and any **GPS** section.\n3. If you see GPS data, notice the button at the bottom: **Remove Properties and Personal Information**.\n\n*Hint:* photos sent through some chat apps may have no GPS section, because the app removed it.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Metadata is data about data, and it can reveal more than the content.\n- Photos can carry EXIF data, including exact GPS location. Remove it before sharing.\n- Browser fingerprinting combines many small facts into an ID; incognito mode does not stop it.\n- Give apps only the permissions they really need, and prefer \"while using\" and \"approximate\".\n- Data minimisation: collect and share only what is needed. Data never collected can never leak.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is EXIF data, and name two things it can contain.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**EXIF** is hidden metadata stored inside a photo file. It can include the **camera model**, the **date and time** taken and the **GPS location**.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nConvert this GPS value to decimal form: 24° 28' 12\" N. Use decimal = degrees + minutes ÷ 60 + seconds ÷ 3600.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n24 + 28 ÷ 60 + 12 ÷ 3600 = 24 + 0.46667 + 0.00333 = **24.47**\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYou are building a sign-up form for a neighbourhood book-swap club. Plan it with privacy in mind.\n\n1. List the fields you **need**, and explain why.\n2. List three fields you would **not** collect, and explain why.\n3. Write one sentence for the form telling members how their data is used.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **Needed:** first name (to greet members), email (to send swap reminders), a password (to log in).\n2. **Not collected:** home address (swaps happen at the library), date of birth (not needed), phone number (email is enough). Less data means less to protect and nothing extra to leak.\n3. \"We use your email only to send book-swap reminders. We never share it, and you can delete your account at any time.\"\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-incident-response-and-capstone/ch14-incident-response-and-forensics.md": "# Chapter 14: Incident Response & Digital Forensics 🚨\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nEvery school practises fire drills. When the alarm rings, nobody panics or argues about what to do. Each class knows its exit, teachers take the register, and one person calls the fire service. Afterwards, the head teacher asks: \"What went well? What should we change?\" The plan was written **before** any fire. Security teams work the same way. They expect that one day something will go wrong, and they prepare a calm, step-by-step plan for that day.\n\nAn **incident** is any event that harms, or threatens, the security of a system: a stolen password, a malware infection, a leaked file. **Incident response** is the plan for handling it. **Digital forensics** is the careful collection of evidence to understand what happened.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- List the six phases of incident response and what happens in each.\n- Explain why evidence must be protected, and why logs matter.\n- Respond correctly to a common student incident: a secret key pushed to GitHub.\n- Report a weakness you find in someone else's system, responsibly and honestly.\n\n## ⏱️ The Six Phases of Incident Response\n\nSecurity teams often follow six phases taught by the **SANS Institute**, a well-known security training organisation. **NIST**, the US standards agency, describes very similar steps.\n\n```\n1. Preparation\n      │\n      ▼\n2. Identification\n      │\n      ▼\n3. Containment\n      │\n      ▼\n4. Eradication\n      │\n      ▼\n5. Recovery\n      │\n      ▼\n6. Lessons Learned ──► back to 1\n```\n\n### Phase 1: Preparation\nEverything you do **before** anything goes wrong. Keep backups (Chapter 12). Turn on logging. Write down who to call and who is in charge. Practise.\n\n### Phase 2: Identification\nNotice that something is wrong, and confirm it. Signs include an alert from security software, many failed logins, or a user reporting a strange message.\n\nA **log** is a file where a system records events, one per line. Even a simple script can spot a warning sign in a log. This one counts failed logins (it uses `str_contains()`, which checks whether one string contains another):\n\n```php\n<?php\n$log = [\n    '2026-10-01 08:01 LOGIN_OK   user=aisha',\n    '2026-10-01 08:02 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n    '2026-10-01 08:02 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n    '2026-10-01 08:02 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n    '2026-10-01 08:03 LOGIN_OK   user=bilal',\n    '2026-10-01 08:03 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n];\n\n$fails = [];\nforeach ($log as $line) {\n    if (str_contains($line, 'LOGIN_FAIL')) {\n        $user = substr($line, strpos($line, 'user=') + 5);\n        $fails[$user] = ($fails[$user] ?? 0) + 1;\n    }\n}\nforeach ($fails as $user => $count) {\n    echo \"$user: $count failed logins\\n\";\n    if ($count >= 3) {\n        echo \"ALERT: check account '$user'\\n\";\n    }\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nadmin: 4 failed logins\nALERT: check account 'admin'\n```\n\nFour failed logins in two minutes on the admin account is worth checking. Without logs, nobody would ever know.\n\n### Phase 3: Containment (stop it spreading)\nLimit the damage quickly.\n\n- **Disconnect** the affected device from the network: unplug the cable or turn off Wi-Fi.\n- **Do not switch it off straight away** if experts may investigate. A computer's working memory (**RAM**) is wiped when power is lost, and it may hold clues about what was running.\n- Lock or reset affected accounts.\n\nFor a home or school device, the simple version is: **disconnect, do not delete anything, and tell an adult.**\n\n### Phase 4: Eradication (remove the cause)\nFind **how** the attacker got in and close that gap. Remove the malware. Fix the bug. Change every password or key that might have been seen.\n\n### Phase 5: Recovery (return safely)\nRestore clean systems and data from backups made **before** the incident. Watch closely for a while in case the problem returns.\n\n### Phase 6: Lessons Learned\nMeet and ask: How did it happen? What worked? What will we change? Keep this meeting **blameless**: the goal is to fix the system, not to shame a person. People who fear blame hide mistakes; people who feel safe report them early.\n\n## 🔍 Forensics: Protecting Evidence\n\n**Forensics** means collecting evidence carefully, so the story of what happened can be trusted.\n\n- Write down **what you saw and when**: times, messages, screen text.\n- Take photos or screenshots of error messages.\n- Do not \"tidy up\" by deleting files. That destroys evidence.\n- Experts copy disks and then work only on the copy. They record a **hash** (Chapter 10) of the copy, so anyone can later check it was not changed.\n\n## 🔑 A Common Student Incident: A Secret on GitHub\n\nSara pushes her project to a public GitHub repository. Later she notices that `config.php` contains her database password and an API key (an **API key** is a secret code that lets a program use an online service).\n\n| ❌ Risky response | ✅ Correct response |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Delete the file in a new commit and hope nobody saw it | **Revoke** (cancel) the key and change the password at once. Automated bots scan public repositories for secrets within minutes |\n| Tell nobody | Tell the teacher or project owner |\n| Keep secrets in code files | Move secrets to a file listed in `.gitignore`, such as `.env`, and only ever commit an example file without real values |\n\nDeleting the file is not enough: the old commit still contains the secret in the project's history. The key must be treated as stolen.\n\n## 📜 Reporting a Weakness Responsibly (*Ṣidq*)\n\nWhat if you notice a security problem on someone else's site, such as your school portal?\n\n1. **Stop.** Do not explore further, and do not look at other people's data. Exploring without permission is illegal, even with good intentions.\n2. **Write down** simply what you noticed and how, without trying it again.\n3. **Report privately** to the owner: a teacher, the IT office, or the contact listed in the site's `/.well-known/security.txt` file, if it has one.\n4. **Keep it private** while they fix it. Organisations often ask for up to 90 days.\n5. **Be truthful** (*Ṣidq*): report exactly what you did and saw. Honesty is what separates a trusted defender from an intruder.\n\n## 🏠 What to Do at Home\n\nYour family's mini incident plan:\n\n- Know who to tell: a parent, or the IT office at school.\n- If a device acts strangely: disconnect it from the internet, do not delete anything, tell an adult.\n- If an account is taken over: use the service's official recovery page, change the password and turn on 2FA.\n- Change passwords from a **different, healthy** device.\n- Keep backups ready (Chapter 12) so recovery is possible.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"My laptop has malware! I will quickly delete the strange files and reinstall everything.\"\n\n**What happens:** the strange files might be the only clue to **how** the malware got in. Without knowing that, the same hole stays open and the problem returns. Hurried deleting can also wipe your own work that was not backed up.\n\n**Fixed version:** disconnect first, write down what you see, tell an adult or IT staff, and let them decide what to collect before cleaning.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nWrite a one-page incident plan for a student coding club.\n\n1. Who is told first if something goes wrong?\n2. What are the first three things to do if an API key is pushed to a public repository?\n3. Where are the backups, and who can restore them?\n\n*Hint:* follow the six phases in order, starting with Preparation.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Incident response has six phases: Preparation, Identification, Containment, Eradication, Recovery, Lessons Learned.\n- Logs make incidents visible; without them, attacks go unseen.\n- Contain first (disconnect), protect evidence, then remove the cause.\n- A secret pushed to a public repository must be revoked at once; deleting the file is not enough.\n- Report weaknesses privately and honestly; never explore further without permission.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nList the six phases of incident response in order.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Preparation\n2. Identification\n3. Containment\n4. Eradication\n5. Recovery\n6. Lessons Learned\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nPut each action into the correct phase:\n\n1. Restoring the website from last Tuesday's clean backup.\n2. Unplugging the infected computer's network cable.\n3. Writing down who to call if the school website is defaced.\n4. Removing the outdated plugin that let the attacker in.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. **Recovery**\n2. **Containment**\n3. **Preparation**\n4. **Eradication**\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nChange the log script above so it:\n\n1. Also counts successful logins for each user.\n2. Prints an alert only when a user has **3 or more** failed logins **and no** successful login.\n\nTest it with the same log and write down the output.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```php\n<?php\n$log = [\n    '2026-10-01 08:01 LOGIN_OK   user=aisha',\n    '2026-10-01 08:02 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n    '2026-10-01 08:02 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n    '2026-10-01 08:02 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n    '2026-10-01 08:03 LOGIN_OK   user=bilal',\n    '2026-10-01 08:03 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin',\n];\n\n$fails = [];\n$oks = [];\nforeach ($log as $line) {\n    $user = substr($line, strpos($line, 'user=') + 5);\n    if (str_contains($line, 'LOGIN_FAIL')) {\n        $fails[$user] = ($fails[$user] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n        $oks[$user] = ($oks[$user] ?? 0) + 1;\n    }\n}\nforeach ($fails as $user => $count) {\n    if ($count >= 3 && empty($oks[$user])) {\n        echo \"ALERT: $user has $count failed logins and no success\\n\";\n    }\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nALERT: admin has 4 failed logins and no success\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-incident-response-and-capstone/ch15-capstone-building-an-impenetrable-community-portal.md": "# Chapter 15: Capstone: Building an Impenetrable Community Portal 🏰\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAn old fort was never safe because of one giant wall. It was safe because of many things working together: a moat, a strong gate, guards who checked every visitor, a locked treasury, and a well inside in case of a long siege. Even then, wise commanders never called a fort \"impenetrable\". They kept checking the walls and kept improving them. In this capstone you will build that kind of fort for a website. \"Impenetrable\" is our goal, not a promise: no real system is perfect, but many strong layers make it very hard to break.\n\nCongratulations on reaching the final chapter! You will now bring everything together in one security plan.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Build a **threat model**: what needs protecting, from whom, and what could go wrong.\n- Apply every layer from this book to one real-world style project.\n- Write secure PHP for logins, queries, output and forms.\n- Add security **headers** and check a site against a final audit list.\n\n## 🎯 The Project: Bayt Al-Amanah\n\n**Bayt Al-Amanah** (a made-up charity) runs a website that coordinates food-bank deliveries, interest-free community loans (*Qard Hasan*) and help with medical costs for families in need.\n\n### The threat model\nA **threat model** is a short plan that answers four questions.\n\n| Question | Bayt Al-Amanah's answer |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| What are we protecting? | Family names and addresses, medical notes, grant records, volunteer logins |\n| Who might attack? | Fraudsters making fake claims; people wanting personal data; automated bots |\n| What could go wrong? | Stolen passwords, injection, one family seeing another's records, a leaked database |\n| What will we do? | The six layers below |\n\nEvery decision below protects real people. That is the meaning of *Amānah*.\n\n## 🔐 Layer 1: Logins and Passwords (Chapter 2)\n\n- **Password rule:** at least 12 characters, passphrases encouraged, no forced symbols. Check new passwords against a list of common passwords.\n- **Storage:** Argon2id through `password_hash()`. Each hash gets its own salt automatically.\n\n```php\n<?php\n$options = ['memory_cost' => 65536, 'time_cost' => 4, 'threads' => 1];\n$hash = password_hash('lamp orbit cedar mild frost', PASSWORD_ARGON2ID, $options);\necho substr($hash, 0, 31), \"\\n\";\nvar_dump(password_verify('lamp orbit cedar mild frost', $hash));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n$argon2id$v=19$m=65536,t=4,p=1$\nbool(true)\n```\n\nThe start of the hash records the settings: 65,536 KiB (64 MiB) of memory, 4 passes and 1 thread. The rest is the salt and the hash itself.\n\n- **Slow down guessers:** after 5 failed logins in 15 minutes, pause that account and ask for extra checks.\n\n```php\n<?php\nfunction tooManyAttempts(array $failTimes, int $now): bool {\n    $recent = 0;\n    foreach ($failTimes as $t) {\n        if ($now - $t < 900) {   // 900 seconds = 15 minutes\n            $recent++;\n        }\n    }\n    return $recent >= 5;\n}\n\n$now = 10000;\nvar_dump(tooManyAttempts([9100, 9500, 9800], $now));\nvar_dump(tooManyAttempts([9200, 9300, 9400, 9600, 9900], $now));\nvar_dump(tooManyAttempts([1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 9900], $now));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nbool(false)\nbool(true)\nbool(false)\n```\n\nOnly the second list has 5 failures inside the last 15 minutes. Old failures from long ago do not count.\n\n- **MFA:** every staff member and volunteer uses an authenticator-app code as well as a password.\n\n## 🗄️ Layer 2: The Database (Chapters 7 and 8)\n\n- **Every** query uses prepared statements. No user input is ever glued into SQL.\n- **Every** query for personal data checks ownership or role.\n\n```php\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\n    \"SELECT * FROM grant_applications WHERE id = ? AND family_id = ?\"\n);\n$stmt->execute([$_GET['id'] ?? 0, $_SESSION['family_id']]);\n$application = $stmt->fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);\n```\n\n- **Least privilege:** the website's database account may `SELECT`, `INSERT` and `UPDATE` the application tables only. It cannot `DROP` (delete) tables or create new users.\n- **Medical notes** are encrypted in the database (Chapter 10), with the key stored outside the code.\n\n## 🖥️ Layer 3: Pages and Forms (Chapters 5, 6 and 8)\n\n- **Validate** every input on the server (type, length, allowed values).\n- **Escape** every output:\n\n```php\nfunction e($value) {\n    return htmlspecialchars($value, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');\n}\necho '<p>' . e($volunteerNote) . '</p>';\n```\n\n- **CSRF token** in every POST form, checked with `hash_equals()`.\n- **Error pages** show a friendly message only. Details go to a private log.\n\n## 🌐 Layer 4: Security Headers and HTTPS (Chapters 6 and 11)\n\n**Headers** are extra instructions the server sends with every page. In PHP they are sent with `header()` before any page content:\n\n```php\n<?php\nheader(\"Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'\");\nheader(\"Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains\");\nheader(\"X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\");\nheader(\"Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin\");\n```\n\n| Header | What it does |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `Content-Security-Policy` | Allows scripts only from this site; `frame-ancestors 'none'` stops other sites showing this page inside a hidden frame (a trick called **clickjacking**) |\n| `Strict-Transport-Security` (HSTS) | Tells browsers to use HTTPS only, for 2 years (63,072,000 seconds) |\n| `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` | Stops the browser guessing a file's type, so a text upload is never run as a script |\n| `Referrer-Policy` | Shares less of the page address when visitors follow a link to another site |\n\nThe session cookie is set like this:\n\n```http\nSet-Cookie: session_id=...; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\nOlder guides use `X-Frame-Options: DENY` to stop clickjacking. It still works, and you may send both.\n\n## 🧩 Layer 5: Software and Secrets (Chapters 5 and 12)\n\n- Keep PHP, the database and every library **updated**. Remove plugins nobody uses.\n- Keep passwords and keys in a `.env` file that is listed in `.gitignore`, never in code.\n\n## 🚨 Layer 6: Logs, Backups and a Plan (Chapters 12 and 14)\n\n- Log logins, failed logins, permission errors and admin actions, without logging passwords.\n- Daily backups following 3-2-1, with a test restore every month.\n- A written incident plan: who is called, who can switch the site off, who tells families.\n\n## 🌟 The Trustworthy Guardian (*Al-Amīn*)\n\nBefore his prophethood, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was known in Makkah as **Al-Amīn**: the trustworthy one. People left their valuables with him because they knew he would protect them.\n\nWhen you:\n\n- choose prepared statements instead of shortcuts,\n- hash passwords with slow, salted algorithms,\n- check permissions on every request,\n- and collect only the data you truly need,\n\nyou are keeping an *Amānah* and doing your work with *Iḥsān*: excellence and care, even in places nobody else will see.\n\n## 🎓 Textbook Completion Milestone\n\nCongratulations! You have completed all 14 chapters of **Cybersecurity & Digital Hygiene**. You are ready to explore the **Cybersecurity Lab Studio**, try the practice exercises, and protect the people who trust the websites you build. Use your knowledge only to defend, and always with permission.\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mistake:** \"We added a firewall and HTTPS, so the portal is secure. We can stop now.\"\n\n**What happens:** HTTPS protects data on the way, and a firewall blocks some traffic. Neither one stops a missing ownership check, an unescaped comment or a reused admin password. Attackers look for the one layer you forgot.\n\n**Fixed thinking:** go through **every** layer in this chapter, and check them again after every new feature. Security is a habit, not a single task.\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nTake a PHP project you have built, or the project you are building now.\n\n1. Write a four-line threat model using the table above.\n2. Go through Layers 1 to 6 and mark each item ✅ done, ⚠️ partly done or ❌ missing.\n3. Fix one ❌ today.\n\n*Hint:* start with the highest risk, usually prepared statements or ownership checks.\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A threat model asks: what are we protecting, from whom, what could go wrong, and what will we do?\n- Strong logins: long passphrases, Argon2id or bcrypt, limits on guessing, and MFA.\n- Safe data: prepared statements, ownership checks, least privilege and encryption for sensitive notes.\n- Safe pages: validate input, escape output, CSRF tokens and security headers.\n- Updates, secrets kept out of code, logs, backups and an incident plan complete the fort.\n- No system is perfect; many layers, checked often, make it very hard to break.\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nName one defence from this chapter for each of these risks:\n\n1. A stolen password.\n2. SQL injection.\n3. A comment that contains HTML.\n4. One family viewing another family's application.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. **MFA** (a code from an authenticator app).\n2. **Prepared statements.**\n3. **Escaping output** with `htmlspecialchars()`.\n4. An **ownership check** in the query (`AND family_id = ?`).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nExplain what each part of this header does:\n\n```http\nStrict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains\n```\n\nHow many days is 63,072,000 seconds?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- `Strict-Transport-Security` tells browsers to use **only HTTPS** for this site.\n- `max-age=63072000` means \"remember this rule for 63,072,000 seconds\".\n- `includeSubDomains` applies the rule to every subdomain too, such as `volunteers.example.org`.\n\n63,072,000 ÷ 86,400 seconds per day = **730 days** (2 years).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nRun a full security audit of a \"Volunteer Sign-Up\" page for Bayt Al-Amanah. The page has a form (name, email, phone, available days), saves to a database, and shows a list of volunteers to coordinators.\n\n1. Write a threat model (four questions).\n2. List at least eight checks across the six layers.\n3. Write the PHP that saves a sign-up safely: CSRF check, validation, and a prepared statement.\n4. Write the PHP line that shows one volunteer's name safely.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **Threat model:** protecting volunteers' names, emails and phone numbers; from spammers, bots and people collecting contact details; risks are injection, XSS, fake sign-ups and a leaked list; defences below.\n2. **Checks:**\n   - HTTPS and HSTS on every page.\n   - CSRF token in the form.\n   - Server-side validation of every field.\n   - Prepared statement for the insert.\n   - Output escaping on the coordinators' list.\n   - Only logged-in coordinators (role check) can see the list.\n   - Coordinators use MFA.\n   - Limit sign-ups per hour to slow bots.\n   - The database user cannot drop tables.\n   - Daily backup and a log of who viewed the list.\n3. Saving a sign-up:\n\n```php\n<?php\nsession_start();\nif (!hash_equals($_SESSION['csrf_token'] ?? '', $_POST['csrf_token'] ?? '')) {\n    http_response_code(403);\n    exit('Form expired. Please reload and try again.');\n}\n\n$name  = trim($_POST['name'] ?? '');\n$email = filter_var($_POST['email'] ?? '', FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL);\nif ($name === '' || strlen($name) > 60 || $email === false) {\n    exit('Please enter your name and a valid email.');\n}\n\n$stmt = $pdo->prepare(\"INSERT INTO volunteers (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)\");\n$stmt->execute([$name, $email]);\necho 'Thank you for volunteering!';\n```\n\n4. Showing a name:\n\n```php\necho htmlspecialchars($volunteer['name'], ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');\n```\n\n</details>\n"
};
